// Erzeugt von tools/build-konstellationen-en.mjs aus src/doppelspalt/src/data/en/. Nicht von Hand bearbeiten.
// Die 326 Konstellationen von ORNA auf Englisch: je ID text und question; Namen und Adressen wie im Original.
export const constellationsEn = {
 "tehching-hsieh__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "Tehching Hsieh binds his life to a rule and lets time pass bodily. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun, by contrast, examines digital media as systems that remain stable through repetition, habit and permanent updating. For both, repetition produces no return to the same moment: the next punch on Hsieh's time clock uses up lifetime; the next update reproduces a network under changed conditions. Hsieh makes this consumption visible on the body, whereas digital media largely conceal it behind interfaces. In each case the rule stabilises a space of possible operations within which updating must take place again and again. Performance and platform thus move surprisingly close together.",
  "question": "What would become visible if digital repetition were perceived as consumed lifetime, as it is with Hsieh?"
 },
 "dan-graham__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "Dan Graham builds situations in which people observe themselves and others observing; mirrors, glass and video delay make the present spatially and temporally unstable. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun describes networks, habits and updates as the conditions of digital presence. With Graham, one's own observation returns as an image; in the network, past behaviour returns as profile, recommendation or expectation. Both turn the traces of earlier operations into conditions of the next. Graham, however, exhibits this process as a perceptible experimental set-up, whereas on platforms it runs largely at the level of infrastructure. What is called a profile there might appear here as a time-delayed mirror.",
  "question": "How does self-observation change when the mirror already calculates ahead?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "Louise Bourgeois builds rooms, bodily forms and objects in which the past can become present again. Gregory Bateson asks when a difference within a context makes a further difference. Bourgeois' recurring forms can therefore be read as differences whose meaning changes with their respective context. The Cells stabilise spatial arrangements, but the event of perception cannot be held fast within them. Bateson's concept of pattern explains how relationships remain recognisable across repetitions; Bourgeois shows how bodily different such a recurrence can be experienced. Memory would then be neither merely a stored state nor pure repetition, but a present reorganisation of differences.",
  "question": "Where is a memory located if it only becomes a difference in being perceived anew?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__niklas-luhmann": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt separates the rule of a work from its particular material execution. Niklas Luhmann correspondingly distinguishes between structures, which restrict possibilities, and operations, which can only ever take place in the present. A Wall Drawing can therefore remain the same even though its concrete realisation passes away and is executed anew in another place. The instruction spatialises possibilities; each execution actualises only one of them. Yet the concrete drawing is not simply contained in the rule; it has to be produced. The work thus exists strangely between a repeatable structure of decisions and an unrepeatable act of execution.",
  "question": "What exactly remains identical when a work exists only by being produced anew again and again?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "Felix Gonzalez-Torres lets works shrink, disappear and be replenished without their thereby ceasing to be the same works. George Spencer-Brown begins with a simpler operation: a boundary is drawn and thereby produces a marked and an unmarked side. With Gonzalez-Torres, the boundary of the work likewise seems precisely determined and at the same time materially mobile. A sweet can leave the installation, be eaten and disappear, while the identity of the work persists. The form, then, does not necessarily stabilise itself through the identity of its components. Spencer-Brown's boundary prompts the question of where the relevant distinction actually runs here: between work and non-work, rule and material, presence and absence?",
  "question": "How much can a form lose and still remain the same?"
 },
 "christian-marclay__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "Christian Marclay's The Clock assembles thousands of filmic depictions of clock times so that they coincide with the actual time of the audience. Gregory Bateson understands information not as a thing but as a difference that makes a difference within a context. A clock time shown on screen is therefore at first only a difference; only its relation to the viewer's present time makes it an event. Marclay spatialises enormous quantities of past film time in an assembled archive and temporalises this archive again through its screening. The same excerpt thereby acquires a different operative meaning at 14:37 than outside this synchronisation. Past and present do not become identical, but are for a moment related to one another.",
  "question": "What exactly do we perceive when a depicted time suddenly designates our own time?"
 },
 "donald-judd__michel-serres": {
  "text": "Donald Judd arranges bodies and intervals with great metrical precision. Michel Serres, by contrast, also thinks space through paths, neighbourhoods and transitions. With Judd, the distance between two objects remains measurably the same, while their relationship changes as one walks. What stands side by side becomes a sequence of nearness and distance only through the path of the body.",
  "question": "Is nearness a question of distance or of the path?"
 },
 "bruce-nauman__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "Bruce Nauman repeats movements, words, gestures and video sequences until their apparent identity becomes unstable. Jacques Derrida describes iterability precisely in that something must be repeatable in order to function as a sign, yet inscribes itself with each repetition in a new context. Nauman's loops make this shift bodily perceptible. The technical recording can return identically; the perceiving body does not. What was stabilised spatially or in a medium therefore produces a sequence of different presents. With Nauman, Derrida's trace almost acquires a body.",
  "question": "Where does the repetition lie if only the recording stays the same?"
 },
 "james-turrell__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "James Turrell builds spaces in which light does not merely make something visible but in which seeing itself becomes conspicuous. Hannah Arendt describes the public as a space of appearance that arises when people become visible and audible to one another. Turrell produces this space technically and architecturally; Arendt understands it as the relational condition of acting together. Neither, then, treats appearing as a self-evident property of existing things. Something can appear only if conditions exist under which it can appear. The difference is decisive: with Turrell this concerns, first of all, perception; with Arendt, a world brought forth in common.",
  "question": "What happens to a common world when even the conditions of its appearing are unequally distributed?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "William Kentridge draws, erases and draws again, while earlier states remain on the paper as smeared traces. Donna Haraway turns against the view from nowhere and insists that knowledge arises from concrete, situated positions. Kentridge's sheet likewise no longer possesses an innocent initial state: each new drawing arises on the material consequences of earlier decisions. The place from which work continues already carries its history with it. Haraway's situatedness here acquires a temporal dimension; position would be not only a where but also an after-what. At the same time, the animation makes this irreversible history technically replayable.",
  "question": "Can a place be determined without taking its history into account?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin stabilises seeing through grids, repetition and minimal deviation. Elena Esposito examines how communication deals with a future that does not yet exist but becomes effective in the present through expectations, probabilities and forecasts. Martin's grid seems at first to do the opposite: it reduces possibilities to an almost unchanging order. Yet it is precisely this stability that makes the smallest shifts in perception noticeable. With Esposito, models stabilise expectations without being able to fix the coming present. In both cases an order creates the conditions for something to appear that is not yet contained within it.",
  "question": "How much order does the unforeseeable need in order to appear as change at all?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__judith-butler": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin repeats lines and grids; Judith Butler examines how norms become effective through repetition. In both cases repetition produces stability without ever simply bringing forth the same thing. For Butler, a norm can persist only by being enacted again and again, and precisely therein lies the possibility of its displacement. Martin's grid seems unchanging, yet it is perceived anew with every glance and is materially different in every execution. The decisive difference lies in the site of this repetition: with Butler it organises social legibility, with Martin a situation of perception.",
  "question": "When does repetition stabilise an order, and when does it begin to tip precisely through its repetition?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__niklas-luhmann": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven inscribes time in numbers, dates, series and sheets. Niklas Luhmann, by contrast, does not describe time as something that could be stored anywhere: operations happen and vanish; only present operations can connect to past ones. Darboven's vast arrangements seem to preserve past time spatially. Yet the archive itself becomes present only when someone reads, counts, looks or turns the page. The place on the sheet can be found again; the moment of its making cannot. Storage and recurrence thus come apart.",
  "question": "What do we actually preserve when we say that a past has been stored?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "Eva Hesse produces serial forms whose materials can age, sag, discolour and decay. Michel Foucault examines orders that distribute bodies, things and statements to particular places and thereby make them visible and comparable. Hesse's works seem at first to offer such orders themselves: series, repetitions, intervals. Yet their material does not reliably keep to the order it was given. What appears as a spatial system begins to develop a history of its own. Foucault makes visible how orders shape bodies; Hesse prompts the question of what happens when material answers its order with ageing, gravity and deformation.",
  "question": "How long does a body belong to an order whose form it no longer keeps?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović frequently reduces performances to a few rules, a particular place and a present body. Susan Leigh Star examines infrastructures and boundary objects that make collaboration possible even though the participants understand them differently. A performance, too, requires such an infrastructure: space, institution, schedule, audience, rules and documentation must come together before the seemingly immediate encounter can take place. But whereas infrastructure works especially well when it becomes invisible, Abramović makes presence demonstratively visible. The body at the centre can thereby conceal the many conditions that organise its presence in the first place.",
  "question": "What do we see when we look at the infrastructure of the performance?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "Rebecca Horn extends bodies with feathers, rods, masks and machines, thereby altering reach, distance and touch. Claude Shannon, by contrast, abstracts communication into message, channel, signal and statistical selection. The body almost entirely disappears from his model. Horn could at first be read as a reversal of this abstraction: with her, the channel itself becomes bodily. An extension of the arm does not simply transmit a movement from A to B; it changes what the body can reach and perceive. The material form of the channel becomes part of the event.",
  "question": "What remains of the concept of transmission when the channel changes what can be perceived at all?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "Mona Hatoum takes familiar things and spaces and alters their scale, materials or relations so that familiarity turns into insecurity. Elena Esposito examines how present communication generates expectations about an unknown future. Both begin with something known: a household object, a body of data, a regularity observed so far. Hatoum alters the situation so that the known suddenly no longer offers reliable orientation. Forecasts do almost the opposite: they use the known to make uncertainty manageable again. Here two opposing movements stand side by side. Hatoum produces insecurity from familiar forms. Esposito asks how expectations arise out of uncertainty.",
  "question": "What do we perceive when the forms with which we reduce uncertainty themselves become uncertain?"
 },
 "on-kawara__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "On Kawara paints a date and makes a past day retrievable as an unambiguously designated place. Michel Foucault examines archives as orders that do not simply store everything past but determine what can become findable, distinguishable and assignable as a statement. Kawara's date paintings work like elementary archives: a day is given an address. At the same time, almost everything that happened on that day disappears. It is precisely the utmost precision of the date that produces an enormous blank. The archive thus preserves not the past itself but an order under which something past can be addressed again.",
  "question": "How much must be forgotten for a past moment to receive an unambiguous address?"
 },
 "donald-judd__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "Donald Judd organises bodies, intervals and repetitions with great spatial precision. Henri Bergson distrusts precisely the operation by which time is treated like a series of points lying side by side. For him, duration is not a sequence of interchangeable places but qualitative change, in which earlier moments continue to work within later ones. Judd's serial objects seem at first to stand on the other side: one beside the other, measurably separate. Yet a viewer can never perceive this series from all positions at once. He must move, remember and carry previous views into the next.",
  "question": "Does a wholly spatial order inevitably give rise to a time as soon as someone begins to perceive it?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "William Kentridge draws, erases and draws on; Claude Shannon describes information as a selection from a defined space of possible messages. For Shannon, it must be clear which alternatives are possible before information content can be calculated at all. Kentridge's sheet seems precisely not to hold such a space of possibilities in readiness. Each drawing alters the surface on which the next will arise; even erasing does not restore the earlier state. The possibilities of the next stroke thus depend on an irreversible history of the material. What appears mathematically as a selection among possibilities becomes, with Kentridge, a selection that alters its own space of possibilities.",
  "question": "How can a possibility be determined when every decision changes what is still possible afterwards?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin produces pictures from lines, intervals and repetitions whose differences often emerge only on close perception. N. Katherine Hayles examines what is lost when information is thought independently of its material carrier. Martin's grid could easily be described as an abstract pattern and stored as a sequence of intervals or coordinates. But that would not yet explain what happens when pencil, paint, canvas, light and a perceiving body come together. It is precisely the small irregularities of her lines that show that a structure is not identical with its material embodiment. Hayles thus shifts the question from pattern to its embodiment, Martin from the grid to its perception.",
  "question": "What is lost when a perceived form can be fully described as information?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "Mona Hatoum alters familiar objects and spatial situations so that orientation can turn into irritation. Claude Shannon, by contrast, must determine for his theory of information precisely which possible signals can be distinguished and transmitted. A channel works only if differences can be determined within a defined system. Hatoum's artistic operation takes hold at a different point: it is not necessarily the signal that changes but the situation within which something is perceived as familiar, dangerous, near or far. Shannon can measure that a difference has been transmitted; that does not yet determine what this difference means for a body.",
  "question": "Where does meaning begin when a technically determinable difference suddenly alters a bodily situation?"
 },
 "robert-smithson__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "Robert Smithson shifts material between site and exhibition and, with Site and Nonsite, makes the difference between a place and its representation itself the subject; at the same time he is preoccupied with displacement and entropy. Hannah Arendt is interested in how a common world comes into being in which people and things appear and relationships can gain duration. With Smithson, however, a place precisely cannot be transferred completely into this common space: the stone in the museum is at the site of its exhibition and at the same time points to another place. The world then consists not simply of things in their places but also of relations between present and absent places. Through Smithson, Arendt's space of appearance acquires, as it were, a geological disturbance.",
  "question": "Can something be present in common whose decisive place remains absent?"
 },
 "roman-opalka__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "Roman Opalka's sequence of numbers has the simplest conceivable plan: after the next number comes the next. Lucy Suchman, however, distinguishes between plans and situated action: an order can orient action without anticipating its concrete execution. With Opalka, the symbolic continuation can be determined exactly, but the execution is bound to hand, attention, fatigue and lifetime. The plan knows which number should follow; it does not know under what bodily conditions it will be painted. The predictability of the series and the openness of the event thus come apart.",
  "question": "How much future can a plan contain if its execution only becomes real in a situation?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "George Spencer-Brown begins with a distinction: a boundary is drawn, and with it two sides arise. Sol LeWitt frequently also begins with a formal stipulation, an instruction, a grid, particular lines or rules of combination. What becomes decisive is what happens to this stipulation in execution. LeWitt himself stresses that different executors understand the same instruction differently and that even the same person does not realise the same plan identically twice; plan and execution nonetheless remain related to one another. Spencer-Brown makes the operation of distinction formally determinable, whereas LeWitt lets us observe what happens to a determination as soon as it has to be executed. The rule stabilises a space of possibilities, but it does not fully contain its concrete actualisation.",
  "question": "Where does the form lie: in the rule or in its execution?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__gilles-deleuze": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin repeats grids, lines and intervals without making identical pictures out of them. Gilles Deleuze sets repetition against the same at exactly this point: what recurs returns under changed conditions. Martin's works test this thought in a surprisingly direct way. The grid produces a strong identity of order, while material, the movement of the hand, light and perception bring forth differences. Without the recognisable order, however, these differences would hardly be experienceable as differences of the same thing. Martin thus leaves open whether difference precedes repetition or only becomes visible through it.",
  "question": "How much sameness does a difference need in order to be perceived as a difference?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven translates calendar time into numbers, writing, series and vast spatial arrangements. Donna Haraway insists that knowledge never comes from nowhere but remains bound to bodies, positions and concrete situations. Darboven's notations seem at first to detach time precisely from such situations: a date is given an abstract, retrievable place. Yet each sheet was written at a particular place and moment and is read anew by a particular body. The universal order of the calendar and the situated act do not coincide.",
  "question": "What changes when a date also has a place of writing?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__gilles-deleuze": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven repeats numbers, dates and operations of writing over decades. Gilles Deleuze would ask precisely in such repetition about the production of difference. Darboven's procedure is strict enough for deviations not to appear as mere arbitrariness. Each new entry belongs to the same operation and yet occupies a place that no other entry can occupy. The work thus makes difference visible not through spectacular change but through almost compulsive recurrence. The more similar the operation, the more clearly it emerges that no moment returns.",
  "question": "Does repetition need a rule in order to produce difference at all?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven organises her work through systems, calculations and recurring procedures. Lucy Suchman criticises the idea that a plan could already contain how an action will actually unfold. A plan provides resources for a situation; someone must first act in that situation. Darboven's works bring this difference onto paper: the rule can persist, but every page must actually be written. Between system and sheet lie time, labour and a body.",
  "question": "What does the execution of a rule add that cannot be present in the rule itself?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven produces archives of her own operations through continued writing. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun examines digital cultures in which systems are at once changed and stabilised through updates and habits. Both combine continuation with repetition, but under almost opposite material conditions. Darboven's past operations remain visible as sheets; digital systems frequently overwrite earlier states precisely by updating themselves. The new can thus become the technique by which continuity is produced.",
  "question": "How do systems remember that constantly renew themselves?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "Eva Hesse's works can be described formally, but their materials prevent a completely detached gaze. Donna Haraway understands knowledge as situated and thus bound to concrete conditions of observing. Latex, fibreglass, gravity and ageing are, with Hesse, not incidentals that could be factored out of the description. They help change what can be recognised of the form at all.",
  "question": "What does material change about our standpoint?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "Eva Hesse's sculptures often look less like completed bodies than like provisionally stabilised material states. Gilbert Simondon likewise understands individuals not as finished unities but as results of continued individuation. This proximity must not, however, obscure the fact that Hesse's work has to remain identifiable as this work even when its material ages. The process thus meets a limit that makes Simondon's thinking of process particularly interesting: museums have to decide which changes still belong to the history of a work and which endanger its identity.",
  "question": "When is change the continuation of a form, and when does something else come into being?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__heinz-von-foerster": {
  "text": "Eva Hesse repeats forms without eliminating the differences of their material execution. Heinz von Foerster examines how recursive processes can bring forth stable eigenforms. Hesse's series seem to offer such stability and undermine it at the same time. A form recurs, but material, position and perception prevent complete identity. Stability would then not be immutability but something that is recognised despite change.",
  "question": "How much change can a form absorb and still recur as the same?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "N. Katherine Hayles criticises the idea that information could exist independently of its embodiment. Eva Hesse's works push this question into the material: a form in latex is not simply the same information in a different substance. Ageing, elasticity, transparency and gravity belong to what is perceived. One could measure Hesse's form digitally with exactness and still not store what happens to it over decades. Here the difference between describable structure and material eigentime shows itself.",
  "question": "Which properties of a work can change carrier, and which disappear precisely in that change?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "George Spencer-Brown begins with a boundary that divides a form into two sides. Louise Bourgeois, with her Cells, builds spaces whose boundaries can at once separate, protect, enclose and let the gaze pass through. With Spencer-Brown, the boundary is a formal precondition of distinction; with Bourgeois, it is experienced bodily. One stands before it, walks around it, looks in and remains outside. The marked and the unmarked side thereby acquire position, material and duration.",
  "question": "What happens to a distinction when its boundary itself has to be perceived?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__heinz-von-foerster": {
  "text": "Louise Bourgeois returns over decades to forms, memories and conflicts without simply reproducing them. Heinz von Foerster describes recursion as an operation whose results can in turn become conditions of further operations. In this sense Bourgeois' recurrence could be understood less as a cycle than as recursive change. An earlier work or a memory is not retrieved but observed anew from a later present. The recurrence thereby also changes what appears as past.",
  "question": "Can one return to something without at the same time changing that to which one returns?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "Louise Bourgeois often makes absence present through spaces, objects and bodily forms. Jacques Derrida's concept of the trace likewise designates no simple presence of the past: something is effective in the present precisely because what it points to is not fully present. Bourgeois gives this paradoxical structure material, weight and space. A garment, a bed or a locked cell can address something without bringing it back. Yet the perceived object is itself fully present.",
  "question": "Can an absence be perceived, or do we only ever perceive something that designates it in the present?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović often alters a few conditions of a situation and observes what arises from this between body, audience and surroundings. Gregory Bateson understands information relationally: a difference becomes relevant only where it makes a further difference within a context. A minimal movement in a performance can therefore become more significant than a large change outside its rules. The situation helps decide which difference becomes noticeable at all. Abramović, however, adds a vulnerable body to Bateson's abstract relation.",
  "question": "When does a bodily change become information for another body?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__judith-butler": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović explicitly calls her works performances. Judith Butler, however, means something else by performativity: actions become socially legible because they connect to already existing norms. Abramović can determine the rules, duration and risk of a situation, but she cannot determine from scratch how her body is read within it. The performance is singular; its social legibility has a longer history.",
  "question": "What about a performance is legible beforehand?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__karen-barad": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović can reduce a situation to the point where body, objects, space and audience become unusually dependent on one another. Karen Barad disputes that observer and observed always already precede such situations as fully separate entities. An apparatus first brings forth certain boundaries and determinabilities. Abramović seems to supply an ideal example of this and at the same time a problem: her body remains vulnerable even if its meaning arises relationally. A cut can bring forth roles, but it cannot arbitrarily undo what has happened bodily.",
  "question": "Which boundaries arise only in the situation?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__michel-serres": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović uses the body not only as a depicted motif but as the medium of a situation with the audience. Michel Serres is interested in the fact that transmission never simply passes from one point to another: channels contain noise, detours and mediations. A performance, by contrast, seems to reduce mediation as far as possible; body and audience share place and time. Yet precisely this apparent immediacy needs space, rules, attention and bodily limits. Presence, too, has a channel.",
  "question": "What does immediate encounter mean if even presence needs conditions for its transmission?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "Henri Bergson distinguishes lived duration from a time that is broken down into measurable units and lined up side by side like places. Marina Abramović often works with fixed periods, repetition and bodily endurance. A performance can be described in hours, days or rules; but that does not capture how this time passes for body and audience. It is precisely the measurable framing that makes a second time noticeable: fatigue, expectation, boredom, concentration and change possess no uniform units. The spatialisation of time stabilises the experimental set-up, whereas its temporalisation takes place only in the irreversible act.",
  "question": "What does a statement of duration measure, and what begins only where the measured time has to be lived through?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "Rebecca Horn joins bodies to feathers, rods, masks and machines. Donna Haraway's cyborg disturbs precisely the clear boundary between organism and technology. Horn's extensions do not simply illustrate this figure. They show bodily that reach, touch and action belong neither to the body alone nor to the apparatus alone.",
  "question": "When does a technical extension become part of the body?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__heinz-von-foerster": {
  "text": "Heinz von Foerster makes the observer a component of observing. Rebecca Horn adds a changeable body to this abstract figure. Her apparatuses intervene in the conditions under which distance, movement and touch become possible. What changes is thus not merely the observed but the apparatus of observing itself. The observer cannot simply leave her own position; she can, however, rebuild its conditions.",
  "question": "Can one observe differently without thereby also becoming a different observer?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__michel-serres": {
  "text": "With Rebecca Horn, connections arise through extensions, machines, feathers, rods and moving bodies. Michel Serres is interested in passages, but equally in the noise and the change that accompany every passage. A prosthesis seems at first to bridge a distance. At the same time it produces a new distance: touch now takes place through something that steps between body and world. Connection and separation arise in the same operation.",
  "question": "Does an extension bring us closer to something, or does it produce a new form of the in-between?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "N. Katherine Hayles criticises the idea that information could be detached from its material embodiment without something decisive being lost. Mona Hatoum makes familiar forms lose their self-evidence precisely through material, scale and spatial arrangement. An object can remain unambiguously recognisable as a form and yet become something entirely different for a body. Its geometry could be stored and transmitted; weight, temperature, resistance, nearness and possible danger arise only in a concrete embodiment. Hatoum thereby makes visible that the same describable structure can bring forth different situations of perception.",
  "question": "What remains of a form when its material becomes interchangeable?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "Hannah Arendt describes a common world as something that at once connects people and separates them from one another, comparable to a table around which different positions are gathered. Mona Hatoum repeatedly transforms precisely familiar things and spatial orders into precarious situations. The common object then guarantees no common experience. People can stand before the same thing and yet be affected by it in completely different ways. Spatial commonality and shared world come apart.",
  "question": "What makes a space common when the people in it cannot appear under the same conditions?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__judith-butler": {
  "text": "Judith Butler examines how bodies become recognisable and legible as particular bodies within social norms. Mona Hatoum often tips familiar orders so that an object or space loses its accustomed legibility. This irritation concerns not only meaning but the body that has to relate to it. Something can look familiar and yet demand a different movement, distance or caution. Perception and social legibility thereby come apart.",
  "question": "What happens to a norm when the body has to act differently?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "Doris Salcedo works with objects and architectural interventions in which past violence remains present without simply being depicted. Gilbert Simondon understands forms not as final states but as provisional resolutions of tensions. Salcedo's works, by contrast, seem precisely to stabilise tensions that have found no resolution. Furniture, walls or fissures hold fast something that cannot be concluded. Form would here be less a resolution than continued material unrest.",
  "question": "Can a form stabilise something precisely by preventing a tension from disappearing?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "Gregory Bateson's famous definition of information demands a difference that makes a further difference. Doris Salcedo often works with absences whose effect consists precisely in the fact that something is no longer there. A blank can therefore make a difference even though it is not itself a present object. This works, however, only within a context in which the absence becomes noticeable at all. The context makes what is missing effective.",
  "question": "Can absence be information?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "Henri Bergson understands the past not as a collection of filed-away points in time but as something that continues to work within present duration. Doris Salcedo gives past losses material places in the present. Her works, however, do not make the past present again, as if it could be retrieved. Rather, an object carries a difference that time has not cancelled. Bergson's duration and Salcedo's material memory thus meet at a difficult boundary between continued effect and representation.",
  "question": "How can the past be effective in the present without becoming present again?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__niklas-luhmann": {
  "text": "Niklas Luhmann treats the past as a construction available in the present: what has happened cannot return, but can only be remembered and designated through present operations. Doris Salcedo confronts this formal severity with material, body and loss. A work cannot repeat past violence and cannot bring back the lost person. But it can establish present conditions under which the absence receives attention anew. Communication does not take over the past event; it organises a new present around it.",
  "question": "What exactly recurs when the event itself remains unrepeatable?"
 },
 "on-kawara__gilles-deleuze": {
  "text": "On Kawara repeats the same elementary operation over decades: painting a date, on the day designated, according to strict rules; if the painting is not finished by midnight, it is destroyed. Gilles Deleuze separates repetition from the mere occurrence of the same. Kawara seems to test this distinction almost experimentally. The rule remains stable, while each execution designates a day that can never occur again. It is precisely the extreme similarity of the paintings that makes their temporal non-identity visible.",
  "question": "Is it the repetition of the form that lets us recognise that the moment never repeats itself?"
 },
 "on-kawara__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "On Kawara's date paintings are radically unambiguous: each designates exactly the day on which it was made and must be completed within that day. Jacques Derrida, by contrast, draws attention to the fact that a sign can function precisely because it survives its original moment of origin and becomes legible again in other contexts. Kawara's date enacts exactly this paradox. 25 SEP. 2026 could designate a unique day and at the same time be read again decades later. The moment passes; its designation becomes repeatable.",
  "question": "What remains of a moment when only its sign lives on?"
 },
 "on-kawara__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "On Kawara makes days addressable: date, format and rules transform unique lifetime into an ordered series. The Today paintings follow fixed formats and rules of production, while each individual day remains unrepeatable. Susan Leigh Star examines classifications and infrastructures that make heterogeneous realities comparable and coordinable. The calendar is one such powerful infrastructure: billions of different experiences can be filed under the same date position. Kawara uses this order without claiming that it contains the day in question.",
  "question": "How much difference must a classification ignore for different events to be able to share the same place at all?"
 },
 "on-kawara__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "On Kawara binds each date painting to a single day and a strict rule of its making. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun examines digital media that produce the impression of a continuous present through permanent updating. Both combine actuality with repetition, but in opposite ways. With Kawara, each day is concluded and becomes irretrievably past; in the digital update, the past is continuously overwritten so that something can appear as present. The one procedure collects concluded presents; the other produces the present through renewal.",
  "question": "What becomes of the past when the present only ever appears as the next update?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "Tehching Hsieh subjects his everyday life to a single rule over long periods. Gilbert Simondon understands individuals not as finished unities but as provisional results of continued individuation. Hsieh's performances place this processual character under unusually rigid conditions. The rule remains the same, while the body that executes it changes with every hour. What is stable, then, is precisely not the individual but the arrangement within which its change takes place.",
  "question": "What becomes visible through a rule that stays the same for longer than the body that follows it?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "Tehching Hsieh repeats actions under rules whose meaning arises precisely from their continued execution. Jacques Derrida describes repeatability as the condition for signs and actions to be able to function beyond their original context. With Hsieh, this repeatability meets lifetime: the next execution can be formally the same, but can no longer take place in the same moment or with the same body. Repetition requires a recognisable form and at the same time produces the trace of its passing.",
  "question": "What does „the same once more“ mean when the once-more has already changed the conditions?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__karen-barad": {
  "text": "Karen Barad directs attention to apparatuses that are not simply neutral environments but help determine which differences can be determined at all. For his performances, Tehching Hsieh builds downright radical apparatuses out of rule, clock, space and body. The time clock does not simply measure an already existing performance; it belongs to the conditions under which this performance can come into being. Yet the body does not remain arbitrarily definable by the experimental set-up: fatigue, ageing and vulnerability have consequences of their own.",
  "question": "Which differences does an experimental set-up produce, and which differences does the body itself bring into it?"
 },
 "donald-judd__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "Donald Judd arranges clearly distinguishable bodies and intervals; Claude Shannon requires clearly distinguishable possibilities in order to determine information mathematically. For both, difference seems at first to be a question of precise positions. Yet Judd's objects are not perceived as an abstract state space: a body moves past them, distances change, views disappear. Shannon gains calculability by removing precisely this concrete perception from his problem.",
  "question": "What is added to a determinable difference as soon as it has to be perceived by someone?"
 },
 "donald-judd__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "Donald Judd's works emphasise particular forms, materials, dimensions and relations in space. Gilbert Simondon, by contrast, is interested in how individuals emerge from processes and can never be fully separated from their coming into being. Judd's industrially fabricated objects at first look like counter-arguments: precisely determined and complete. Yet they too exist only through material, fabrication, architecture, light and the particular situation of perception. The determinate form and the processes of its production can be distinguished, but not fully detached from one another.",
  "question": "When does a process become a thing, and what has to become invisible in the process?"
 },
 "donald-judd__karen-barad": {
  "text": "Karen Barad asks how material arrangements bring forth the boundaries of what can be determined in a situation. Donald Judd builds arrangements in which object, interval, wall, floor and the viewer's position are related to one another with unusual precision. The object alone is therefore not enough to determine what is perceived. At the same time, Judd's concrete materiality resists the idea that the boundaries of the situation are merely effects of its observation. Steel remains steel; distance remains something to be crossed bodily.",
  "question": "Which boundaries are produced by an arrangement, and which confront it as material resistance?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__judith-butler": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's instructions can persist as works only by being executed again and again. Judith Butler likewise describes norms not as rigid things but as orders that remain effective through repeated enactments; performativity is not to be equated with a single performance. With LeWitt, the rule remains recognisable even though each realisation takes place in a different location, by different hands and under different conditions. With Butler, repetition produces social legibility and at the same time opens up possibilities of displacement. A particular time of the rule thus becomes visible: it seems to precede the individual enactment and yet can persist only by being enacted anew.",
  "question": "Can a rule really exist before its executions if it remains effective only through its repetition?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's instruction remains recognisable even though each execution can become concrete in a different place. With transduction, Gilbert Simondon describes processes in which a structure does not simply copy itself but continues step by step under local conditions. LeWitt's rule travels through wall, material, scale and executing person. Its identity shows itself precisely in its ability to bring forth different realisations.",
  "question": "How does a rule travel through different materials?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's work seems to make possible a radical separation of information and material carrier: an instruction can be stored, transmitted and executed anew on different walls. N. Katherine Hayles, however, draws attention to the fact that information never appears without some embodiment. LeWitt's rule, too, must be written, read, understood and materially realised. The wall is therefore not merely an interchangeable surface; it changes scale, bodily relation and perception.",
  "question": "What actually remains invariant when the same information takes on a different body?"
 },
 "roman-opalka__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "Roman Opalka organises his work as a continuous sequence of numbers whose next step is formally predictable. Elena Esposito, by contrast, examines a future that remains future precisely because it cannot be fully known. Opalka's procedure brings the two into clear tension. Which number comes next is certain; whether and when it can actually be painted is not. The order knows its continuation; the body does not know its future.",
  "question": "How predictable is a future that has not yet arrived?"
 },
 "roman-opalka__heinz-von-foerster": {
  "text": "Roman Opalka's next number arises from the preceding one and in turn becomes the precondition of the next. Heinz von Foerster's thinking of recursion describes exactly such operations, whose results re-enter the process. Yet Opalka's recursion has a peculiarity: it cannot return to its starting point. Each step extends the series and at the same time uses up lifetime. Recursion thereby becomes irreversible.",
  "question": "What does recurrence mean when there is no return?"
 },
 "roman-opalka__michel-serres": {
  "text": "Roman Opalka seems to construct a passage from one number to the next that is as free of interference as possible. Michel Serres is interested precisely in what intervenes in a passage: noise, deviation, parasite. With Opalka, this in-between sits in the body itself. Fatigue, voice, hand, age and finally death interrupt a sequence that could mathematically be continued without limit. The interference does not come to the system from outside; it belongs to its material execution.",
  "question": "Does finitude first give an order its history?"
 },
 "roman-opalka__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "Roman Opalka's numbers can easily be noted as abstract information and continued digitally almost without limit. His work, however, does something else: the sequence is bound to a particular body, a hand, a voice and a lifetime. N. Katherine Hayles' critique of disembodied information therefore touches a sensitive point. The numbers can change body; the work cannot do so without further ado.",
  "question": "When does the identity of a work end if its embodiment changes?"
 },
 "roman-opalka__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Susan Leigh Star examines classifications and infrastructures through which things receive stable places and different activities become coordinable. Roman Opalka's sequence of numbers seems to be the most unambiguous classification conceivable: each number has exactly one position. Yet the work binds this abstract order to a finite act of execution. Between two unambiguously determined places there suddenly lies something that no classification can fully absorb: lifetime spent.",
  "question": "What disappears from an order when it can give every event an unambiguous place?"
 },
 "christian-marclay__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "In The Clock, Christian Marclay assembles countless already produced film scenes into a temporal order that meets the actual time only in each particular screening. Lucy Suchman distinguishes between plans and situated action: an order can prepare action but cannot fully anticipate what happens in a concrete situation. Marclay's montage is fixed down to the last detail, yet its relation to the present arises anew every time. 16:23 is prepared in the film; that someone actually sits in front of it at 16:23 is an event. The structure organises the possibility of a coincidence without being able to store its experience.",
  "question": "How much of an event can be prepared before it ceases to be an event?"
 },
 "christian-marclay__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "Christian Marclay reorders a vast film archive according to a single property: the time of day shown. Michel Foucault is interested in the fact that archives do not simply preserve the past but establish orders under which something becomes findable and sayable. Marclay's montage suddenly turns completely different films into adjacent moments. Plot, genre and year of production recede; 14:01 follows 14:00. A new order thereby produces relations that were not present in the original material.",
  "question": "What history does a new order of the archive produce?"
 },
 "christian-marclay__michel-serres": {
  "text": "Michel Serres is interested in transmission, passage and the interferences that arise in them. Christian Marclay works precisely with such transitions: a film cut connects spaces, times and stories that originally had nothing to do with one another. In The Clock, the transition must at the same time produce a strict temporal continuity. The cut separates and connects in the same moment. What is filmically discontinuous can thereby be experienced as a continuous present.",
  "question": "Does continuity arise between events, or only through the operation that connects them?"
 },
 "christian-marclay__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "Christian Marclay produces a present out of already recorded images; Wendy Hui Kyong Chun examines digital presence as the effect of permanent updating. Both thereby call into question the supposed self-evidence of the „now“. With Marclay, the now consists of fragments of past films. In digital media, it arises from stored data, earlier behaviour and ever new updates. The present would then be less the opposite of storage than a particular way of actualising what is stored anew.",
  "question": "How much past does a system need in order to be able to show us something as present?"
 },
 "bruce-nauman__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "Bruce Nauman places bodies, language and actions in loops, repetitions and confined situations. Elena Esposito examines systems that generate expectations about an unknown future from past data. A loop seems at first to be the counter-model to prognosis: instead of opening up the future, it repeats the past. Yet every repetition at the same time generates an expectation of what is about to happen. The longer the repetition lasts, the more predictable the next repetition can become, and the more conspicuous a deviation would appear.",
  "question": "When does repetition become a prognosis?"
 },
 "bruce-nauman__gilles-deleuze": {
  "text": "Bruce Nauman repeats movements, gestures and sentences until their apparent identity becomes unstable. Gilles Deleuze disputes that repetition simply brings back the same. Nauman's body provides a difficult touchstone for this: an instruction can stay the same, but fatigue, attention and movement change. The repetition produces its difference partly through its own execution. Yet without a recognisable action, this change could not be observed as a difference of the same repetition.",
  "question": "Does difference arise despite repetition, or precisely because something remains recognisable as repetition?"
 },
 "bruce-nauman__niklas-luhmann": {
  "text": "Niklas Luhmann describes operations as events that vanish again in the very moment of their execution. Bruce Nauman can technically bring back the same movement as a video loop, seemingly without end. Two different times thus meet. The recording returns; the perceptual event of watching it does not. A technically identical state produces a sequence of non-identical presents.",
  "question": "What actually repeats in a loop if the perception of its repetition can never return?"
 },
 "robert-smithson__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "Michel Foucault examines institutions, archives and spatial arrangements in terms of how they distribute things, statements and visibilities to particular places. Robert Smithson's Site/Non-Site works bring a distant place into such an order through material, map, container and exhibition. The gallery therefore does not simply show the place; it dismantles, addresses and reorganises it under its own conditions. At the same time, the site persists outside the exhibition space and eludes its complete ordering. Smithson thereby makes visible that spatialisation means not only giving things a place but also determining under which order this place becomes observable.",
  "question": "What happens to a place when an institution reorders it?"
 },
 "robert-smithson__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "Henri Bergson criticises a conception of time that treats change like a series of states lying spatially side by side. Robert Smithson, by contrast, works with actual space and at the same time with entropy, that is, with changes that cannot simply be reversed. Spiral Jetty remains spatially locatable and yet changes with water level, salt, material and time. The place remains findable without guaranteeing an identical state.",
  "question": "What does it mean to return to the same place when the place itself has taken on time?"
 },
 "robert-smithson__karen-barad": {
  "text": "Karen Barad makes the apparatus a component of what becomes determinable through an observation. Robert Smithson's Site/Non-Site likewise does not separate cleanly between object and representation: material, map, gallery and distant place refer to one another reciprocally. The exhibition space does not simply show the place but produces a particular possibility of observing it. Nevertheless, the distant place remains outside this arrangement and has a history that the museum does not control.",
  "question": "Where does an apparatus end when what it makes observable continues to happen spatially outside it?"
 },
 "robert-smithson__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "Lucy Suchman stresses that situations are not fully determined by preceding plans. Robert Smithson's interest in entropy confronts spatial planning with a similar limit. A form can be laid out, but material, weather and landscape continue their history. In Spiral Jetty, natural forces change the work continuously. The artistic decision thus produces not a completed form but the conditions of a further history.",
  "question": "When does the execution of a plan end if its materials go on acting after the execution?"
 },
 "james-turrell__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "James Turrell controls light and architecture so precisely that perception itself can become conspicuous. Donna Haraway insists that there is no view from nowhere. Turrell makes this situatedness bodily experienceable: even a different position, adaptation or lighting situation changes what can be seen. Perception thus has a place and a history. Yet the installation at the same time attempts to bring very different bodies into a common situation of perception.",
  "question": "How common can a perception be if every gaze proceeds from a different bodily place?"
 },
 "james-turrell__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "Michel Foucault examines arrangements that organise visibility: who can see whom, what, and from which position? James Turrell seems to reduce this political question to light, architecture and perception. Yet his spaces, too, determine very precisely where someone may stand, where someone may look and what can appear at all. Visibility is thus not merely a property of an object but the result of an arrangement.",
  "question": "How is visibility organised?"
 },
 "dan-graham__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "George Spencer-Brown describes not only the drawing of a boundary but also the possibility that a distinction re-enters the space it has itself produced. Dan Graham's mirrors and video delays make such a return visible. The observer reappears in her own observation, but shifted in time and from a new position. Self-observation is therefore no return to the starting point.",
  "question": "What happens when a distinction comes back upon itself?"
 },
 "dan-graham__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "Hannah Arendt links the public with a space in which people appear before one another. Dan Graham makes this appearing itself observable: mirrors, glass and video let people see themselves being seen. In his time-delay arrangements, one's own past can become part of this situation a few seconds later. To appear then means not only to be visible to others but also to receive one's own visibility back.",
  "question": "How does public action change when we can observe ourselves at the same time from the perspective of our observers?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "Felix Gonzalez-Torres creates works whose material may be taken away by the public and whose stock may later be replenished. Henri Bergson understands duration not as a sequence of interchangeable states but as change in which the past continues to act within the present. Gonzalez-Torres' work can return materially to an earlier weight without thereby returning to an earlier state. The new sweets replace those taken; they do not undo their history.",
  "question": "Can something be restored materially without returning in time?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "Jacques Derrida makes repeatability the condition for something to persist beyond its original context. Felix Gonzalez-Torres pushes this repeatability all the way into the material identity of the artwork: paper may be taken away, sweets may be eaten and stocks may be produced anew. The work remains by its material not having to remain. Its identity lies neither entirely in the individual object nor independently of any material execution.",
  "question": "What must be repeatable for something to recur as the same despite the disappearance of its components?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "Gregory Bateson asks about differences that make further differences within a context. With Felix Gonzalez-Torres, a visitor may actually remove part of the work; the stock changes and can later be replenished. A minimal action thus alters material, appearance, relation to the public and the future possibilities of the work all at once. Not every removal is isolated: it changes the situation for the next person.",
  "question": "When does the action of a single viewer become a condition for the perception of the next?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__niklas-luhmann": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin offers the eye an extremely stable order of lines, intervals and repetitions. Niklas Luhmann distinguishes perception and communication as different operations: communication can presuppose perception and react to it, but cannot carry it out itself. Martin's paintings make this boundary immediately palpable. One can describe grids, dimensions and materials; that does not repeat what someone saw in front of the painting. At the same time, it is precisely the stable form that can trigger ever new events of perception.",
  "question": "How can a perception be communicated when the perception itself cannot be conveyed?"
 },
 "james-turrell__niklas-luhmann": {
  "text": "James Turrell builds situations in which it is not a depicted object but seeing itself that can become conspicuous. Niklas Luhmann's theory of art is interested in the coupling of perception and communication: art does not communicate by taking over the viewer's perception. It provides forms with which perceiving and communicating can continue. With Turrell this difference becomes especially radical, because it is hard to say what «content» was supposed to be transmitted. The installation organises the conditions of seeing; each body must see for itself.",
  "question": "Can an artwork communicate precisely by transmitting nothing and instead letting perception happen?"
 },
 "dan-graham__heinz-von-foerster": {
  "text": "Dan Graham builds situations in which observers receive their own observation back: through mirrors, glass, cameras and time delays. Heinz von Foerster turns this reflexive turn into a theoretical problem: every observation has a blind spot, because it cannot simultaneously and completely observe its own operation. Graham seems to want to catch up with this blind spot by technical means. Yet as soon as my image appears on the monitor, I am already seeing an earlier operation from a new present. Self-observation produces a further observation instead of making the first fully visible.",
  "question": "How often would an observation have to return to itself in order to catch up with the moment of its own observing?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__niklas-luhmann": {
  "text": "William Kentridge draws, erases and draws on. Earlier states disappear and at the same time remain on the sheet as material traces. Niklas Luhmann describes the present as an operation in which the past is already fixed and the future still open. Kentridge's procedure gives this asymmetry a visible surface: the next stroke can change much, but cannot undo what has already been drawn; even erasing leaves traces. The past thus limits the space of further possibilities without determining the next drawing.",
  "question": "How open is the future when the past has already left traces?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__niklas-luhmann": {
  "text": "Felix Gonzalez-Torres lets works diminish materially and be replenished, while institutionally they can continue to exist as the same works. Niklas Luhmann describes identity not necessarily as unchanged substance but as something that is stabilised across changing operations for further connections. This fits astonishingly well, and yet a difference remains. The museum can decide that a renewed installation is the same work; the eaten sweet does not thereby return. The identity of the work and its material history follow different times.",
  "question": "Who or what decides which changes an identity may survive?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__karen-barad": {
  "text": "Mona Hatoum alters scale, materials and spatial relations so that familiar objects can lose their self-evident meaning. Karen Barad draws attention to the fact that properties and boundaries do not always exist independently of the material arrangements in which they become determinable. Hatoum's works make such a dependency bodily experienceable: the same recognisable object can, through size, material or position, establish an entirely different relationship to the body. The situation changes not only its interpretation but the possibilities of proximity, movement and touch.",
  "question": "Which properties belong to an object, and which arise only between object, arrangement and perceiving body?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "Doris Salcedo gives loss and absence material places without being able simply to declare the experiences of others present. Donna Haraway demands of knowledge that it take its own position and limitation into account. With Salcedo this demand becomes particularly delicate: how can a work refer to an experience that neither the artist nor the public can take over? Material can create a place for memory, but cannot replace the standpoint of the absent.",
  "question": "How can an experience become public without its strangeness disappearing in the process?"
 },
 "christian-marclay__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "In The Clock, Christian Marclay constructs a present out of already recorded pasts. Elena Esposito examines the reverse operation: present data are used to generate expectations about a still unknown future. Between the two lies the same operative moment, the present. Yet it is opened in opposite directions. Marclay lets stored images coincide with the now; forecasting lets stored data point to a possible afterwards. The past is in one case synchronised and in the other extrapolated.",
  "question": "How does forecasting change our present?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt can formulate a work as an instruction that other people execute in other places. Lucy Suchman disputes that a plan already contains the concrete action that will emerge from it. A LeWitt instruction thereby becomes an almost ideal experiment. It must be precise enough for different executions to count as realisations of the same work, and open enough for its concrete shape to arise only on site. Plan and situation do not compete; they need each other.",
  "question": "How precise must an instruction be for its execution still to remain an event?"
 },
 "robert-smithson__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "In his Site/Non-Site works, Robert Smithson separates and connects landscape, material, map and exhibition space. Susan Leigh Star examines boundary objects that can circulate between different social worlds without meaning the same thing everywhere. Smithson's Non-Site could almost be such a boundary object: in the museum it is artwork, material container, map and reference to another place all at once. Its stability does not arise from all these perspectives becoming identical.",
  "question": "How can an object connect different places and meanings without cancelling their differences?"
 },
 "james-turrell__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "Claude Shannon abstracts information from its meaning and treats distinguishable signals within a determinable space of possibilities. James Turrell reduces too, but in the opposite direction. Instead of excluding perception, he reduces objects and images to the point where differences of light, space and seeing itself can become conspicuous. Shannon gains determinability by methodically removing the perceiving body; Turrell gains perceptibility by precisely organising its conditions. Both need distinguishable states, but only one must be able to explain how they appear for a body.",
  "question": "When does a difference between states become a difference in perception?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__karen-barad": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin's grid seems merely to subdivide an already existing surface. Karen Barad would not treat such a division as a mere image of pre-given differences. A boundary can itself take part in what appears as unit, interval or deviation. Martin's lines therefore do not just show differences; they produce the conditions under which differences become visible at all.",
  "question": "Which differences does the grid itself first produce?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven turns days into numbers, writing and long sequences. N. Katherine Hayles reminds us that information never appears without a material form. Darboven's notation can be read as a system, yet handwriting, paper, rhythm and working time belong to the work just as much. What seems abstractly transferable remains bound to a concrete execution.",
  "question": "What is lost when only the information remains?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "Eva Hesse designs clear serial orders and uses materials whose future cannot be fully controlled. Elena Esposito examines how expectations deal with a future that is still open. With Hesse, a planned form meets ageing, gravity and chemical change. The work has a structure, but no definitively predictable state.",
  "question": "How open does a planned form remain?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "Louise Bourgeois makes memory spatial without smoothing it into a general history. Donna Haraway demands a knowledge that knows its own standpoint. In the Cells, things therefore do not appear as neutral evidence of a past. They are bound to body, biography, place and direction of gaze, and yet remain accessible to others.",
  "question": "How does a personal memory become legible for others?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović often lays down few rules and leaves much to the actual encounter of bodies. Lucy Suchman understands action as situated coordination that takes shape in the course of a situation. The audience's posture, gaze, waiting and reaction are therefore not merely an external accompaniment to the performance. They help to change what the action becomes in that moment.",
  "question": "When does the audience become part of the action?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Rebecca Horn extends bodies with apparatuses, feathers, rods and machines. Susan Leigh Star is interested in infrastructures that enable actions and are often overlooked for precisely that reason. With Horn this mediating layer comes to the fore. The connection between body and surroundings becomes visible because the technical in-between itself becomes part of perception.",
  "question": "What becomes visible when mediation no longer disappears?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "Mona Hatoum alters familiar objects so that habit suddenly no longer offers reliable orientation. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun describes habit as a force that keeps technical and social orders stable. Hatoum intervenes at exactly this point. When a table, a kitchen utensil or a room no longer functions as expected, habit itself becomes noticeable.",
  "question": "What do we see only when habit fails?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__judith-butler": {
  "text": "Doris Salcedo works with things and spaces that bear traces of violence, without re-enacting past experiences. Judith Butler asks how bodies become legible and vulnerable through repeated social orders. Salcedo's works show no abstract vulnerability. They give its consequences weight, material and place without replacing the absent body.",
  "question": "How does vulnerability become visible when the body is missing?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "Tehching Hsieh binds his life over long periods to publicly declared rules. Hannah Arendt understands the public as a space in which actions appear before others and become part of a common world. Hsieh's bodily duration remains his own experience, yet contract, documentation and exhibition make it publicly observable. Time spent in private thereby acquires a social form.",
  "question": "When does lived time become public?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__karen-barad": {
  "text": "William Kentridge draws, erases, photographs and draws again. Karen Barad's concept of intra-action does not presuppose that already finished actors meet one another. With Kentridge, too, the emerging movement cannot be cleanly attributed to a single agent. Hand, charcoal, paper, eraser and camera together form the conditions for what can appear as the next figure.",
  "question": "Where does the action lie between hand, paper and camera?"
 },
 "bruce-nauman__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "Bruce Nauman forces bodies into corridors, repetitions and unfamiliar situations of looking. Donna Haraway contradicts the idea of a gaze without location. Nauman's spaces make this situatedness bodily palpable. Where one stands, how one moves and what one's own body cannot see at that moment become part of the work.",
  "question": "What changes when one's own standpoint becomes conspicuous?"
 },
 "dan-graham__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "Dan Graham plans reflections, glass surfaces and video delays very precisely. Lucy Suchman shows, however, that an arrangement does not determine how people actually behave within it. With Graham, the work therefore also arises from reactions, glances and movements that are not in the plan beforehand. The construction is precise; the situation remains open.",
  "question": "What arises only between plan and action?"
 },
 "on-kawara__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "On Kawara paints a date only on the day it designates. Elena Esposito examines a future that can be expected in the present but not known. Kawara's rule separates these two sides sharply. Tomorrow's date is known as a possibility, but as a painting it can come into being only once the day has really become present.",
  "question": "When does a future date become present?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__judith-butler": {
  "text": "Felix Gonzalez-Torres lets works persist through removal, consumption and renewed production. Judith Butler describes identity not as a fixed core but as something that remains effective through repeated enactments. With Gonzalez-Torres, the work remains recognisable although its material components change. Its identity lies in a regulated repetition, not in unchanged substance.",
  "question": "How much change can the same identity bear?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin's grids divide the surface into regular intervals. Henri Bergson warns against treating lived time like a series of uniform positions. In front of Martin's paintings the two orders meet. The grid stays calm while seeing, attention and memory change in the course of looking.",
  "question": "How does duration change a seemingly identical picture?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven makes time comparable with numbers, dates and series. Claude Shannon describes information as a selection from a determinable space of possibilities and deliberately leaves meaning aside. Darboven's notations can be counted and ordered, yet their daily production carries more than information. Time appears here also as work, handwriting and a day spent.",
  "question": "What is missing when time appears only as information?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__michel-serres": {
  "text": "Eva Hesse's works combine clear repetition with materials that do not fully submit to it. Michel Serres is interested in interference, noise and change along the path of a transmission. With Hesse this interference does not sit outside the work. Ageing, gravity and material tension alter the form from within.",
  "question": "When does material become interference?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__niklas-luhmann": {
  "text": "Rebecca Horn uses technical extensions to change what a body can reach and perceive. Niklas Luhmann separates perception and communication as different processes. Horn's apparatuses can order movements and produce situations, but they do not perceive in place of the body. It is precisely this boundary that makes visible how much mediation is needed without perception becoming transferable.",
  "question": "What can an apparatus mediate without perceiving?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "Doris Salcedo gives loss a material trace without making what is lost present again. Jacques Derrida describes the trace as the effect of an absence that does not simply disappear. Salcedo's furniture, cracks and blocked spaces make this tension bodily experienceable. What is missing is not replaced, but it changes the present.",
  "question": "How can a trace show absence?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "Louise Bourgeois binds memory to fabrics, furniture, bodily forms and rooms. N. Katherine Hayles criticises the idea that a pattern could be fully preserved independently of its embodiment. With Bourgeois, memory therefore cannot be reduced to an abstract story. Material, scale and spatial proximity help determine how it becomes present.",
  "question": "What does material contribute to memory?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović repeats actions under strict rules until habit and strain become visible. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun examines how repetition produces habits and thereby keeps orders stable. With Abramović this stability comes at a price. The body must execute the rule anew each time and changes in doing so.",
  "question": "When does repetition become habit?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Mona Hatoum lets familiar rooms and objects become uncertain. Susan Leigh Star shows that infrastructure usually becomes noticeable only when something no longer works smoothly. Hatoum uses exactly this moment. A familiar object loses its self-evidence and makes visible the spatial rules on which we usually rely.",
  "question": "What becomes visible when the familiar no longer works?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "Hannah Arendt does not understand a common world as a place of identical perceptions. Something becomes common precisely because the same thing can appear from different positions. Agnes Martin's grid offers an unusually stable form for such difference. The picture remains the same while the attention, distance and experience of its viewers diverge.",
  "question": "Can the same picture hold different points of view together?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "Louise Bourgeois returns again and again to earlier experiences, figures and rooms. Henri Bergson does not understand memory as a stored image that is retrieved unchanged. The past acts anew out of each present. Bourgeois' Cells show exactly this movement: memory is given a form again, but never the same present.",
  "question": "How does the present change our memory?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__karen-barad": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven orders time through calendars, numbers and writing. Karen Barad asks how an apparatus helps determine what can appear as a difference at all. Darboven's procedure therefore does not simply register time. It fixes which aspects of a day become retrievable as number, date or sign and which remain outside this order.",
  "question": "What does a notation make visible?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Eva Hesse's works age in a way that confronts museums with practical decisions. Susan Leigh Star examines infrastructures that enable stability in the background. With Hesse this infrastructure becomes especially clear, because conservation, storage and exhibition take part in how a changeable work continues to exist. The sculpture and its institutional care cannot be fully separated.",
  "question": "Who holds an ageing work together?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović exposes her own body to a situation and makes its presence the material of the work. Donna Haraway demands a gaze that does not deny its bodily and spatial position. Abramović shows that even a seemingly simple rule does not mean the same experience for different bodies. Presence is always situated.",
  "question": "How does the body change the same rule?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "Rebecca Horn's prostheses change what a body can do and perceive. N. Katherine Hayles links the posthuman not with the disappearance of the body but with the critique of the fully autonomous subject. With Horn, action is distributed across organism, mechanism and surroundings. The body is not replaced, but its capacities no longer belong to it alone.",
  "question": "Who acts when body and apparatus can act only together?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "Mona Hatoum's rooms often seem familiar until a small shift changes movement within them. Lucy Suchman shows that acting does not simply follow a plan but takes shape in the situation. A floor plan can stipulate where things stand. How a body orients itself within it, hesitates or steps aside is decided only on the spot.",
  "question": "What is decided only in crossing a room?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "Elena Esposito examines how the present deals with a future whose outcome is still open. Doris Salcedo works at the other end of this temporal order, with consequences of violence that can no longer be reversed. What was once possibility, decision or risk appears in the work as fixed past. The open future and its irreversible consequence suddenly lie side by side.",
  "question": "When does a possibility become irreversible past?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin's grids live on repetition, without every glance seeing the same thing. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun describes habit as a repetition that becomes self-evident and thereby orders perception. Martin's paintings can slow down this self-evidence. The more familiar the grid becomes, the more readily small deviations stand out.",
  "question": "Can habit make differences more visible?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__judith-butler": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven repeats rules of writing and counting over long periods. Judith Butler shows that repetition does not simply mean copying but is what keeps an order effective in the first place. Darboven's rule therefore does not exist independently of her sheets. It gains its duration by being executed again and again and becoming materially different in the process.",
  "question": "How does execution change a rule?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "Hannah Arendt gives the things of the common world meaning because they can outlast human lives. Artworks possess a special duration in this sense. Eva Hesse chooses materials that undermine precisely this permanence. Her works remain part of a common world although their substances age and their form cannot be secured indefinitely.",
  "question": "How durable must art be in order to form a world?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__karen-barad": {
  "text": "With the Cells, Louise Bourgeois builds boundaries that one looks through, walks along and does not simply cross. Karen Barad understands boundaries not as self-evidently given but as the result of concrete arrangements. With Bourgeois this production becomes bodily experienceable. Inside and outside arise through material, gaze and position.",
  "question": "How do an inside and an outside arise in a Cell?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović's performances can be documented, described and re-performed, but no recording takes over the original bodily enactment. N. Katherine Hayles distinguishes abstract information from its embodiment. With Abramović this difference becomes especially clear. The rule can be transmitted; the lived duration cannot.",
  "question": "What does a documented performance lack?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "Elena Esposito stresses that forecasts do not know the future but generate expectations in the present. Rebecca Horn's machines, by contrast, seem to fix a sequence in advance. Yet as soon as mechanics meet body, friction and space, the intended sequence becomes an actual event. The machine makes the future more calculable without turning it into present.",
  "question": "What does a machine know about its next moment?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "Mona Hatoum alters familiar things so that one's own body has to reassess its position. Donna Haraway reminds us that every gaze is bound to a location. Hatoum's works make this dependency palpable. Proximity, distance and safety look different when one's own position changes.",
  "question": "How does location change what seems familiar?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Doris Salcedo makes loss visible in furniture, walls and public interventions. Susan Leigh Star asks about the often invisible structures that carry a shared practice. Public remembering, too, needs such conditions. Institutions, places, care and rituals help decide whether a material trace can persist and be visited again.",
  "question": "What infrastructure does memory need?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin's grid gives the gaze a stable order but no fixed route. Lucy Suchman describes acting as something that develops under local conditions. Seeing, too, follows no ready-made plan of the picture. Eyes wander, attention breaks off, distance changes and individual lines emerge only later.",
  "question": "Can a picture plan the gaze?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven turns private working time into sheets, series and archives that become publicly accessible. Hannah Arendt thinks of a common world as something that gains permanence between different people. Darboven's notations thereby exceed their original moment of writing. An individual activity becomes an object to which others can later return.",
  "question": "How does private time become part of a common world?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "Eva Hesse's serial forms promise repetition while their materials age and change. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun describes technical stability as the result of continual renewal. With Hesse this logic becomes problematic. Conservation can preserve the work, but every intervention also alters the material course the work has already taken.",
  "question": "When does preservation itself become change?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__judith-butler": {
  "text": "Louise Bourgeois works again and again with bodies, houses, parental figures, sexuality and family roles. Judith Butler examines how bodies and identities become recognisable through social norms. Bourgeois does not simply confirm such categories. Her forms shift, exaggerate and mix what might seem self-evidently feminine, masculine, maternal or bodily.",
  "question": "Which norms do we read into a body?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "In many of her works, Marina Abramović announces duration and conditions in advance. Elena Esposito shows that ideas of the future already change the present, although what is to come has not yet occurred. Whoever knows that a performance will last six hours already experiences its first moment within the horizon of that end. The fixed duration generates expectations without determining the course.",
  "question": "How does an announced end change the present?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__karen-barad": {
  "text": "Rebecca Horn's apparatuses do not simply attach to a finished body. Karen Barad's concept of intra-action draws attention to the fact that relations can help bring forth the sides involved. With a bodily extension, not only the tool changes but also what counts here as reach and bodily boundary. The extended body arises in this connection.",
  "question": "Does the extended body arise only with the apparatus?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "Doris Salcedo binds memory to wood, concrete, clothing and architectural interventions. N. Katherine Hayles warns against detaching meaning from its embodiment. With Salcedo, material is therefore no interchangeable carrier of a message. Weight, damage, surface and spatial blockage themselves contribute to memory.",
  "question": "What does a material know that a text does not say?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin's grids seem open precisely because they do not occupy the gaze with many objects. Donna Haraway reminds us that even a reduced picture is never seen from nowhere. Distance, light, body and attention change what emerges in the grid. Objectivity here does not mean having no location but having a more conscious one.",
  "question": "How does the standpoint change a grid?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven uses calendars, numbers and ordering systems that exist before her individual sheet. Susan Leigh Star examines such classifications as infrastructures of shared work. Darboven turns them into visible material. The calendar no longer appears merely as a neutral aid but as an order that breaks time down into nameable positions.",
  "question": "What does a calendar order, apart from days?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "Eva Hesse works with serial rules, yet her materials follow no perfect plan. Lucy Suchman shows that an instruction becomes effective only in a concrete situation. With Hesse, gravity, material tension and ageing belong to this situation. The executed form is therefore always more than the order that preceded it.",
  "question": "What does material add to a plan?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "Louise Bourgeois returns again and again to particular memories and forms. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun examines habits that become stable through repetition. Bourgeois' return, however, differs from a reassuring routine. The familiar can generate a new tension with every repetition and remain effective precisely because of this.",
  "question": "Can repetition change a memory?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović makes presence before others a component of her performances. Hannah Arendt describes a space of appearance that arises where people become visible and act before one another. With Abramović, the public is therefore not just an audience in the background. The presence of others changes the situation in which the body appears.",
  "question": "What makes presence public?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__judith-butler": {
  "text": "Rebecca Horn alters bodies through extensions, masks and mechanical additions. Judith Butler asks how social norms determine which bodies become legible as self-evident. Horn's apparatuses disturb this self-evidence. They show a body whose boundaries and possibilities are not fixed, without dissolving it into pure arbitrariness.",
  "question": "Which bodily boundaries do we take for granted?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__karen-barad": {
  "text": "Doris Salcedo works with cracks, blocked spaces and damaged objects. Karen Barad asks how arrangements first bring forth particular separations. With Salcedo, a boundary can therefore itself become an event. It does not merely separate two sides but makes loss, exclusion or inaccessibility bodily experienceable.",
  "question": "What makes a boundary palpable?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin's grids function like a silent infrastructure of seeing. Susan Leigh Star is interested in orders that enable actions without constantly standing in the foreground. With Martin this background structure itself becomes the picture. Lines, intervals and repetition carry seeing precisely because they tell almost nothing.",
  "question": "What carries seeing without itself standing out?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven can continue a rule far into the future without having yet executed that future. Elena Esposito examines how present orders generate expectations about what is to come. The next number may be determinable; the next working day is not. Darboven's series therefore hold regularity and open future together at the same time.",
  "question": "How far does a rule reach into the future?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__judith-butler": {
  "text": "Eva Hesse repeats forms without producing identical bodies. Judith Butler understands repetition as an enactment through which an order persists and can change. With Hesse this change is materially visible. Each execution belongs to the series and yet remains a body of its own.",
  "question": "How much difference can a series bear?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "Louise Bourgeois designs the Cells as precise spaces, but she does not prescribe how someone experiences them. Lucy Suchman shows that a situation in use brings forth more than its plan. With Bourgeois, direction of gaze, proximity and movement change what appears accessible or hidden. The spatial order remains; the experience arises in passing through.",
  "question": "What arises only in passing through a Cell?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "Rebecca Horn connects bodies with machines whose movements can repeat. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun examines habits through which technical orders become self-evident. Horn prevents this self-evidence. Her apparatuses make every repetition palpable, because the body has to relate to it anew each time.",
  "question": "When does technology become habit?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "Doris Salcedo brings experiences of loss into a public space without unifying them. Hannah Arendt thinks of a common world as a space between different people. Salcedo's works show that commonality does not presuppose the same experience. A material trace can be shared while the respective memories remain different.",
  "question": "How can memory be shared without becoming the same?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__karen-barad": {
  "text": "Eva Hesse does not separate form and material cleanly from one another. Karen Barad likewise does not understand properties as fully independent of the arrangements in which they emerge. With Hesse, the material changes what the serial form can be at all. Gravity, elasticity and ageing belong to the shape of the work.",
  "question": "Where does form end and material begin?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "Elena Esposito is interested in contingency, in the fact that the present could have turned out otherwise. Louise Bourgeois returns to her past again and again in new forms. The events themselves do not thereby become changeable, but the order in which they are remembered does. Memory can make past possibilities visible again without taking the past back.",
  "question": "Can memory reopen past possibilities?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "Rebecca Horn's machines and bodies do not share the same situation in the same way. Lucy Suchman's work on human–machine interaction shows how easily technical systems and people seem to understand one another although they draw on different information. Horn's apparatuses make this difference bodily. The mechanism executes a movement while the body experiences weight, resistance and meaning.",
  "question": "Do body and machine understand the same movement?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "Doris Salcedo creates material traces that do not have to appear anew through constant updating. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun, by contrast, describes the digital present as something that persists through repetition and update. Salcedo's works set a slower form of duration against this. Their materials remain and change without passing themselves off as new.",
  "question": "Must memory be updated in order to remain effective?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Louise Bourgeois' Cells combine personal things, spatial boundaries and institutional exhibition. Susan Leigh Star examines boundary objects around which different social worlds can work together without everyone seeing the same thing in them. A Cell, too, withstands different readings. Its unity demands no uniform meaning.",
  "question": "How much difference can a shared object withstand?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "Rebecca Horn uses prostheses and machines to change how a body appears in space. Hannah Arendt understands the public as a space in which people become visible before others. Horn's extensions therefore concern not only private bodily experience. They also change the form in which a body faces others.",
  "question": "How does technology change our public appearance?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "Doris Salcedo shapes situations of remembering very precisely, without being able to determine how individual people react to them. Lucy Suchman distinguishes between a planned arrangement and the action that develops within it. With Salcedo this difference remains important. The work can enable an encounter, but cannot prescribe a particular memory.",
  "question": "What can a site of memory not plan?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "Louise Bourgeois makes personal memory publicly accessible in rooms and things. Hannah Arendt asks how a common world can come into being between different people. A Cell does not simply convert private experience into shared knowledge. It places something between the viewers to which each person can build a relationship of their own.",
  "question": "When does personal memory become part of a common world?"
 },
 "on-kawara__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "George Spencer-Brown begins with the drawing of a boundary. Only through it do two sides come into being. On Kawara's date paintings rest on a similarly elementary distinction: this day and all other days. The boundary, however, is temporal. At midnight, today irrevocably becomes a yesterday. Kawara's rule of completing a painting only on the day it names makes this boundary materially effective. The date therefore does not merely designate a place in the calendar. It holds fast the moment at which a temporal distinction became irreversible.",
  "question": "When does a boundary become past?"
 },
 "bruce-nauman__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "Bruce Nauman repeats movements, gestures and sentences until the apparently identical produces differences. George Spencer-Brown describes form as the consequence of a distinction: a boundary separates two sides. Nauman's loops put a simple question to this notion. When the same movement begins again, is the same boundary drawn anew? Body, attention and moment have changed in the meantime. The form can recur, but the situation of its distinction cannot. Repetition would then be no circling within a fixed form, but a continued redrawing of its boundary.",
  "question": "Can the same boundary be drawn twice?"
 },
 "dan-graham__michel-serres": {
  "text": "Dan Graham builds boundaries of glass and mirrors that are interesting precisely because they do not simply close things off. They separate spaces and at the same time let light, gaze and reflection pass through. Michel Serres is interested in passages, and in the fact that mediation never remains neutral. Graham's glass wall is therefore boundary and channel at once. Whoever looks through it does not simply see the other side. Reflection, position and movement alter the relation between here and there. The boundary mediates precisely by separating.",
  "question": "When does a boundary become a channel?"
 },
 "dan-graham__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "Dan Graham's time-delayed video makes the present visible only after it has passed. What I see on the monitor is what I have just done. While this image appears, the expectation of the next one is already forming. Elena Esposito examines how the future becomes effective in the present even though it has not yet occurred. Graham's delay generates exactly this tension: it continuously turns the present into past and thereby opens, at the same time, a horizon of what is about to appear. Here the future arises not despite the delay but through it.",
  "question": "Does delay generate the future?"
 },
 "robert-smithson__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "Robert Smithson's Site and Non-Site exist only through a distinction. Material is located in the exhibition space and at the same time refers to a place outside it. George Spencer-Brown shows that a boundary does not merely separate two sides, but first makes them determinable as sides of a form at all. With Smithson, it is precisely this separation that makes a shared address for something absent possible. Visitors need not enter the distant landscape together. They can refer to it in the museum because Site and Non-Site remain distinguished from and connected to each other.",
  "question": "Can a boundary make an absent place jointly accessible?"
 },
 "robert-smithson__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "Robert Smithson's sites continue to change after an artistic decision has been made. Water, salt, weather and material carry on their history. Gilbert Simondon understands an individual not as a finished form but as the provisional state of an ongoing process. With Smithson, a simple question thereby becomes difficult: what exactly remains identical at a place? The location can be found again, its material condition cannot. Identity might therefore lie less in an unchanged state than in the continued history of a place.",
  "question": "How does a place remain the same when it changes?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "Eva Hesse's materials age, sag, discolour and change their tension. Such changes can be documented as a sequence of individual states. Henri Bergson, however, warns against confusing time with a series of states laid side by side. Duration means that what is past continues to act in the present state. Hesse's material makes this thought almost physically visible. Today's latex does not replace yesterday's. Its present form has emerged from its history so far.",
  "question": "Can a material have duration?"
 },
 "on-kawara__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "On Kawara turns a single day into a lasting object. Hannah Arendt understands the common world as something that can exist between different people and outlast their respective lifetimes. Kawara's date painting, by contrast, begins with a radically personal now. It can come into being on this one day only. Once it is finished, however, precisely this past day can be viewed by many people at different times. A unique now thereby gains a place in a common world.",
  "question": "When does a single day become shared?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's instructions allow different people to execute the same work in different places. Hannah Arendt thinks of a common world not as agreement among all those involved, but as something to which people can refer from different positions. A Wall Drawing connects such positions in an unusual way. Those executing it need not see, think or do the same. Nevertheless their different actions refer to a common rule and bring forth a work that gains permanence between them.",
  "question": "Can a rule bring forth something shared?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "At the start of a performance, Tehching Hsieh lays down what he will do for months or an entire year. A future not yet lived thereby already takes concrete form in the present. Elena Esposito examines how expectations and forecasts make what is to come effective without yet knowing it. With Hsieh, the rule is known, the future experience is not. Fatigue, illness, attention and lifetime have yet to occur. The contract describes a future and, precisely by doing so, already begins to organise the body now.",
  "question": "When does the future begin to act on a body?"
 },
 "james-turrell__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "James Turrell builds situations in which seeing takes time. Eyes adapt, lighting conditions change, and a surface that at first seems empty can gradually gain depth. Elena Esposito examines how expectations make a future effective in the present. With Turrell, too, we therefore do not only see what is present at the moment. We wait for change, reckon with the next moment and direct our attention towards it. The future of seeing is already at work before anything new appears.",
  "question": "When does expectation become part of seeing?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__michel-serres": {
  "text": "Felix Gonzalez-Torres lets parts of his works leave the exhibition space. Paper is taken away, sweets are eaten and stocks are later replenished. Michel Serres is interested in passage, and in the fact that a transmission does not simply carry what is transmitted unchanged from one place to another. With Gonzalez-Torres, this change belongs to the identity of the work. It persists not despite its circulation but precisely through it.",
  "question": "Can passing something on preserve a form?"
 },
 "christian-marclay__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "Christian Marclay's The Clock joins thousands of film fragments into a time that coincides with the audience's clock time. Hannah Arendt describes a common world as something that exists between different people without making their perspectives the same. For this, Marclay provides an unusual common object. Different people see different images, sit in the room for different lengths of time and bring their own memories with them. Nevertheless, for a moment they find themselves in the same edited now.",
  "question": "Can a shared present be cut together?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "Felix Gonzalez-Torres defines works in such a way that their future states remain open. Sweets can disappear and be replenished, stacks of paper be depleted and renewed. Elena Esposito examines how the future is organised in the present without already being fixed. With Gonzalez-Torres, this openness belongs to the identity of the work. The work does not contain its next state, but rules for which changes it can survive in future as the same work.",
  "question": "Does the future belong to the identity of a work?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "William Kentridge works with images in which political history does not appear closed. Drawings are erased and altered, while earlier states remain visible as traces. Hannah Arendt understands the common world as something that gains permanence between people and can at the same time be changed by their action. With Kentridge, the past is therefore neither removed nor simply preserved. It remains as the altered ground of present action. A common world has a history without being entirely determined by it.",
  "question": "How much past does a shared future need?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "George Spencer-Brown begins with a boundary through which two sides come into being. Doris Salcedo works with cracks, blocked passages and things in which separation becomes physically palpable. With her, a boundary therefore does not appear as a neutral line. It can keep a past violence or an exclusion present. The space is divided, and precisely this division remains effective as a trace.",
  "question": "Can a boundary remember?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "With her Cells, Louise Bourgeois builds rooms in which seeing and access are precisely regulated. Michel Foucault examines arrangements according to how they distribute visibility and produce positions. With Bourgeois, one stands before grilles, doors and openings, looks in and at the same time remains outside. The Cell does not only show things. It organises from which point something becomes visible or hidden.",
  "question": "Who may see in a Cell and who remains outside?"
 },
 "on-kawara__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "On Kawara's date paintings follow a strict rule: the painting must be finished on the day it names. If this does not succeed, the rule cannot simply be continued the next day. Lucy Suchman shows that plans only become effective for action in concrete situations. With Kawara, the abstract order of the calendar therefore meets fatigue, working hours and midnight. The situation decides whether the plan becomes a work at all.",
  "question": "When does the situation decide about a rule?"
 },
 "bruce-nauman__michel-serres": {
  "text": "Bruce Nauman lets movements, words and images return in loops. Michel Serres is interested in passage and in the disturbances that arise in the course of it. A loop seems at first to cancel every passage, since it arrives back at its beginning. Yet body, attention and meaning do not return unchanged. Each round carries something of the previous one with it and adds a new deviation.",
  "question": "Can a repetition return without disturbance?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "Hannah Arendt links the common world to things that gain permanence between people. Felix Gonzalez-Torres creates works whose individual components, precisely, need not be permanent. Sweets are eaten, sheets taken away and later replaced. Nevertheless a work remains present to which different people can refer at different times. Duration lies here not in the individual thing but in a form maintained in common.",
  "question": "Can a transient work remain part of a common world?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "William Kentridge draws figures, erases them and continues drawing on the same surface. George Spencer-Brown begins with a boundary through which a form first comes into being at all. With Kentridge, however, a boundary once drawn never disappears entirely. Erasing leaves shadows, smudges and residues that enter into the next drawing. The distinction can be cancelled without restoring an unmarked beginning.",
  "question": "What remains of an erased boundary?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "Michel Foucault examines timetables, repetitions and controls as techniques through which bodies are ordered. Tehching Hsieh subjects himself to extreme rules over long periods. In the time clock performance, every hour becomes a prescribed position for the body. The decisive difference lies in the fact that this discipline is chosen as an artistic rule by the artist himself and made public. Precisely thereby its effect on everyday life becomes visible.",
  "question": "What happens when discipline is self-chosen?"
 },
 "dan-graham__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "Dan Graham's mirrors and video delays feed observations back into the situation from which they arose. Gregory Bateson is interested in feedback loops in which a result becomes the condition of the next step. With Graham, a person does not only see an image of themselves. This image changes how they go on moving and observing. Observation thereby becomes part of a circuit that continuously alters its own situation.",
  "question": "When does observation become feedback?"
 },
 "donald-judd__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "George Spencer-Brown begins with a distinction that separates two sides from one another. Donald Judd's works make clear that the distance between things can accomplish such a separation too. The interval is not merely empty. It keeps bodies apart, determines their relation and gives rise to a sequence of individual forms. Without the distance, the elements would collapse into a different form.",
  "question": "Can a distance generate a form?"
 },
 "bruce-nauman__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "Gilbert Simondon understands individuation as a process in which an individual is not present as finished from the outset. Bruce Nauman repeats bodily movements until posture, rhythm and strain change. The instruction remains recognisable, yet with each pass the executing body becomes a different state of itself. Repetition stabilises the action and at the same time changes the one who performs it.",
  "question": "Does the body become another through repetition?"
 },
 "james-turrell__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "Gregory Bateson describes information as a difference that makes a further difference. James Turrell works with lighting conditions in which the smallest changes become perceptible only after a while. A physical difference alone is therefore not enough. Body, adaptation and spatial context also decide whether it appears as a difference at all. Information arises here at the threshold of perception.",
  "question": "When does a difference in light become information?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "Michel Foucault understands archives not as neutral stores of the past, but as orders under which something can become visible and sayable. William Kentridge takes up historical images, documents and political memories, redraws them and places them in new sequences. History thereby appears not as a finished stock. Its traces are rearranged and acquire different neighbours.",
  "question": "What history does a new order of traces generate?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__gilles-deleuze": {
  "text": "Gilles Deleuze separates repetition from the mere occurrence of the same. Tehching Hsieh's rules drive this distinction to the extreme. In the time clock performance, formally the same action returns every hour, while body, fatigue and lifetime go on irrevocably. The strict sameness of the rule makes visible how different each single execution is.",
  "question": "How much difference does the same hour generate?"
 },
 "dan-graham__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "Gilbert Simondon describes individuation as a process between individual and environment. Dan Graham's pavilions make such a relation immediately visible. Reflection, transparency and standpoint continuously alter what appears as observer, counterpart and environment. The viewing person does not simply stand before a finished situation. Their position arises anew, again and again, together with the spatial arrangement.",
  "question": "Does an observer's position arise only in space?"
 },
 "donald-judd__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "Donald Judd separates bodies by precise distances and makes the interval an essential part of the work. Jacques Derrida uses the idea of spacing not only spatially. For him, identity also arises because something differs from other things and does not fully coincide with itself. Judd's intervals give this abstract difference a concrete spatial form.",
  "question": "Can an interval determine what a thing is?"
 },
 "bruce-nauman__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "Claude Shannon separates the technical transmission of a message from its meaning. Bruce Nauman repeats words and sentences for so long that sound, rhythm and physical exertion can become more important than the original sense. The sequence of signs can remain almost the same while its effect shifts. A stable signal does not guarantee a stable meaning.",
  "question": "Can the same message lose its meaning?"
 },
 "james-turrell__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "George Spencer-Brown begins form with the drawing of a boundary. James Turrell shows how little is sometimes needed for this. An opening, an edge or a change of light zone can suddenly make a seemingly boundless field appear as form. The boundary adds no new object to the light. It makes visible a difference that without it would be perceived differently or not at all.",
  "question": "When does a boundary turn light into form?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__michel-serres": {
  "text": "Michel Serres is interested in translations and passages in which something does not survive the journey unchanged. William Kentridge lets drawing pass over into film, projection, theatre, music and movement. Each change of medium preserves something and at the same time alters rhythm, scale and perception. The work therefore has no neutral channel between its forms.",
  "question": "What changes when the medium changes?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "Tehching Hsieh's time clock performance generates an enormous sequence of clear time stamps. Claude Shannon could treat such distinguishable signs as transmissible information. Yet the stamp does not contain the fatigue, the lack of sleep or the lost freedom of movement that belong to its production. Hsieh makes visible how far a precise date can be from the time spent bodily.",
  "question": "How much life fits into a time stamp?"
 },
 "dan-graham__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "Dan Graham's video delays divide time into precisely measurable seconds. Henri Bergson distinguishes such measurable time from lived duration. In front of the monitor the two meet. Ten seconds can be fixed with technical exactness, while the experience of seeing oneself again, belatedly, cannot be broken down into the same units. The measured delay produces a duration that passes differently from the clock.",
  "question": "How long are ten seconds in perception?"
 },
 "donald-judd__hannah-arendt": {
  "text": "Hannah Arendt gives the things of a common world their significance because they can gain permanence between different people. Donald Judd's objects are designed for durability, material and precise spatial relations. Viewers come and go, while the same bodies and distances become the point of reference again. The work connects different moments of perception through a relatively stable spatial order.",
  "question": "How does a thing establish shared duration?"
 },
 "bruce-nauman__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Susan Leigh Star examines infrastructures that remain invisible especially when they function smoothly. Bruce Nauman often makes the opposite out of an ordinary passage. Narrow corridors, cameras and spatial restrictions make width, access and control suddenly conspicuous. What otherwise merely makes the way possible becomes itself the object of perception.",
  "question": "When does a passage become visible infrastructure?"
 },
 "james-turrell__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "James Turrell's works can create the impression that light appears almost without mediation. Susan Leigh Star, by contrast, turns her gaze to the infrastructures that work in the background of an experience. Architecture, electricity, technical control and spatial shielding belong to the conditions of seeing with Turrell too. Precisely because they hardly attract notice, the light can appear so immediate.",
  "question": "What has to work in the background for light to appear immediate?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__heinz-von-foerster": {
  "text": "William Kentridge draws, erases and continues working on the remains of the previous drawing. Heinz von Foerster describes recursive processes whose results enter again into the conditions of the next operation. Kentridge's sheet works exactly like this, without becoming a closed circuit. Each new drawing responds to a surface that has already been altered by the previous one.",
  "question": "When does an image begin to respond to its own history?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__michel-serres": {
  "text": "Tehching Hsieh devises rules meant to hold, as far as possible without exception, over a long time. Michel Serres is interested in disturbance and in the fact that a channel often becomes visible precisely through its interruptions. With Hsieh, sleep, fatigue, illness and everyday life continually threaten the pure continuation of the rule. The possible disturbance therefore belongs to the performance, even though it is to be avoided.",
  "question": "When does a disturbance make the rule visible?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "Mona Hatoum makes boundaries physically palpable. Grilles, cages and familiar domestic forms can protect, confine or threaten at the same time. Gregory Bateson understands information not as an isolated thing but as a difference that becomes effective within a relation. A boundary therefore does not only separate two areas. It also changes which relations between them are possible. With Hatoum, it is precisely the separation that becomes the carrier of information about proximity, danger and belonging.",
  "question": "When does a boundary become information?"
 },
 "christian-marclay__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "Christian Marclay joins sound, film and images through cuts. George Spencer-Brown likewise begins with a cut that brings forth the two sides of a form. But Marclay's montage shows a second movement: the same cut that separates fragments from one another is what makes their new connection possible in the first place. The boundary does not lie between connection and separation. It produces both at once. Montage would then be a form of joining that draws its force precisely from the visible interruption.",
  "question": "Does the cut connect because it separates?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__michel-serres": {
  "text": "Mona Hatoum's works turn doors, thresholds, grilles and everyday objects into uncertain transitions. Michel Serres is interested in passages and in the fact that transmission never simply leads from one side to the other. Whoever passes a boundary does not necessarily remain untouched by it. With Hatoum, the transition itself can become risky, electric, bodily or political. The boundary is then no quiet edge between two areas, but a place at which what is transmitted changes.",
  "question": "What happens to something when it passes a boundary?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "William Kentridge draws, erases and continues drawing on the same surface. Earlier states do not disappear entirely, but remain effective as shadows and smudges. Gilbert Simondon understands individuation as an ongoing process in which an individual is never fully completed. Kentridge's image therefore has no identity behind its changes. It becomes this image precisely through them. The trace of a past state is not merely memory, but material for further individuation.",
  "question": "Can a trace remain part of identity?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin's grids appear stable, although their lines, intervals and surfaces are never perfectly identical. Gilbert Simondon thinks of individuals not as finished units but as provisional solutions within an ongoing process. Martin's order might therefore appear less as a rigid schema than as the continued individuation of a surface. Line by line, a unity arises whose identity is borne precisely by small deviations. The grid holds together without cancelling every difference.",
  "question": "How much deviation does a stable order need?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "Louise Bourgeois returns again and again to rooms, body parts, parental figures and childhood memories. Gilbert Simondon would not understand identity as an unchanging core lying hidden behind such repetitions. Individuation goes on. Memory, too, would then not only be recourse to something fixed, but material for a later change. With Bourgeois, the past shapes new figures, while these figures in turn change how the past can become present.",
  "question": "Does memory become part of what we become?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Felix Gonzalez-Torres lets works shrink, be replenished, change location and still count as the same work. Susan Leigh Star shows that classifications and infrastructures in the background help decide what can be treated as the same object at all. With Gonzalez-Torres, the identity of the work is therefore not only an aesthetic question. Inventory, certificate, exhibition practice and institutional rules help to read change as continuation.",
  "question": "Who decides when the work is still the same?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's instruction is at first information about a possible action. Gregory Bateson is interested in how information makes a difference only within relations and contexts. An instruction therefore cannot simply be transferred from the text to the wall. Those executing it must read, decide, measure and respond to the situation. Only in this chain does the rule become effective. The work arises not from information alone, but from information being translated into action.",
  "question": "What must a rule communicate in order to become effective?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's instructions can be transmitted like messages. Claude Shannon asks how information passes through a channel, regardless of what it means. This is exactly where LeWitt becomes interesting. A brief instruction can suffice to bring forth very complex works in different places. Yet between transmitted information and visible work lie decisions, material and execution. The instruction can remain technically stable while its embodiment varies.",
  "question": "How much information does a work need?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__elena-esposito": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's instruction exists before its concrete execution is visible. Elena Esposito examines how the future is structured in the present through expectations, programmes and decisions, without thereby already being fixed. LeWitt's rule does something similar. It limits the space of possible future images without anticipating every detail. The work is thus partly determined and partly still open. Its future is organised before it is executed.",
  "question": "How open can a regulated future remain?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "Tehching Hsieh gives an everyday action a different meaning through a publicly fixed frame. Gregory Bateson also described communication in terms of frames that indicate how a behaviour is to be understood. A blow in play is something other than the same blow in a fight. Similarly, with Hsieh, the year-long contract changes what punching a clock, waiting or not speaking means. The rule does not only prescribe a sequence for the action. It marks the context in which each repetition becomes legible as part of the performance.",
  "question": "What makes an action part of a performance?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__michel-serres": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's instructions travel between language, executing persons, wall, tool and space. Michel Serres is interested in passages in which transmission never remains entirely free of loss. Even a precise rule does not arrive at its destination unchanged. Scale, material and local decisions inscribe themselves into its execution. This does not mean that the instruction fails. Rather, its identity shows itself in the fact that it can pass through different translations.",
  "question": "Does an instruction remain the same along its way?"
 },
 "roman-opalka__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "Roman Opalka sets number upon number, marking each time how far the sequence has already reached. George Spencer-Brown begins with a distinction that separates a marked from an unmarked area. With Opalka, beyond the last number written lies a continuation not yet carried out. With each new number this boundary shifts. What was just now the future of the series becomes its fixed past. Counting thus continuously generates a difference between already actualised and still open.",
  "question": "Where does the not-yet-counted begin?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović can communicate the rules of a performance in a few sentences. Claude Shannon's information theory makes it possible to distinguish the transmission of such signs from their meaning. The rule can have arrived completely even though the event it triggers remains undetermined. Pain, hesitation, the audience's reaction and physical exhaustion are not contained in the message. Between transmissible instruction and lived execution a surplus opens up that does not disappear with more information.",
  "question": "What does a completely transmitted rule lack?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "Mona Hatoum's boundaries are rarely mere lines in space. One has to avoid them, wait before them, approach them or put one's own body in relation to them. Henri Bergson distinguishes lived duration from a time that is broken down into individual measurable segments. Hatoum's boundary thereby acquires a temporal side. It is not only seen, but experienced in hesitating, circumventing and crossing. A spatial cut can continue as duration in the body.",
  "question": "How long does a boundary last?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "Tehching Hsieh's performances begin and end at precisely set points in time. George Spencer-Brown describes form as the result of a distinction between two sides. Hsieh's contract draws such a boundary right through the middle of life: inside and outside the performance, before and after. But the boundary does not stay at its beginning. It organises every following day. A cut once set begins to consume real lifetime.",
  "question": "When does a boundary begin to consume time?"
 },
 "christian-marclay__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Christian Marclay's montages presuppose that images and sounds can be found, stored and combined with one another. Susan Leigh Star examines infrastructures precisely where their work disappears behind the visible result. Behind a work like The Clock stand archives, formats, catalogues, playback technology and institutional procedures. The shared present in the exhibition space is carried by a vast invisible order. Montage therefore connects not only fragments, but infrastructures too.",
  "question": "What infrastructure holds a montage together?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven breaks time down into numbers, dates, lines and recurring operations of writing. George Spencer-Brown begins with the drawing of a distinction. A calendar, too, lives from a boundary being set between this day and the next. Darboven multiplies such cuts until a visible order arises from them. Time thereby appears at once as continuous passing and as a sequence of artificially set differences.",
  "question": "What does the cut between two days generate?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__gilles-deleuze": {
  "text": "Mona Hatoum repeats familiar forms such as cage, map, bed or household appliance, but changes scale, material or function. Gilles Deleuze understands repetition not as the return of the identical, but as the production of difference. Precisely for this reason Hatoum's boundaries become unstable. The recurring form does not confirm what a boundary is. It shifts its meaning from protection to threat, from home to exclusion, from order to insecurity.",
  "question": "What does repetition change about a boundary?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "Mona Hatoum's grilles, maps and domestic forms can take on entirely different meanings in different contexts. Jacques Derrida links repeatability to the possibility that a sign is detached from its original context and read differently. A boundary, too, therefore does not remain identical merely because its form recurs. The same fence can mean protection, captivity or political division. Repetition preserves the form and at the same time shifts its legibility.",
  "question": "Can the same boundary remain the same in another place?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__gilles-deleuze": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's rules are repeatable, yet their executions do not entirely coincide. Gilles Deleuze is interested precisely in a repetition that brings forth difference. With LeWitt, this difference does not lie outside the rule. It arises through its renewed application to other walls, dimensions, materials and situations. That an instruction can be repeated therefore guarantees no identical results. The rule produces a family of related but not identical events.",
  "question": "How much difference can the same rule bear?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__heinz-von-foerster": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt often lays down rules by which a sequence of decisions or operations is carried out. Heinz von Foerster is interested in recursion, when results enter again into later operations. Not every LeWitt rule is recursive. But where a line once set changes the conditions for the next, the instruction tips from mere repetition into a process with memory. What has already been executed becomes the precondition of the further course.",
  "question": "When does a rule acquire a memory?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's instructions can be executed again in different places and at different times. Jacques Derrida calls this repeatability iterability. A sign must be able to be detached from its original context in order to be repeatable at all. But precisely thereby its context changes. LeWitt's rule remains recognisable because it can be repeated, and each repetition at the same time exposes it to other spatial and material conditions.",
  "question": "Can a rule be repeated identically?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "Felix Gonzalez-Torres separates the identity of a work from an unchanging material stock. Sweets disappear, paper is taken away and later replaced. N. Katherine Hayles criticises the notion that information can be fully detached from its embodiment. Exactly this tension sustains Gonzalez-Torres' works. The rule or the certificate alone is not the work, but no single material state suffices either. Identity is distributed between information and ever new embodiment.",
  "question": "Where does the identity of a materially changing work reside?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's instructions can break a procedure down into individual steps. Henri Bergson warns against confusing lived time with a series of units laid side by side. Whoever executes a Wall Drawing experiences both at once. The rule orders what is to happen one after another, while the work is experienced as indivisible duration. Planning can structure time, but it does not contain the time its execution actually takes.",
  "question": "Can an instruction order duration?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__heinz-von-foerster": {
  "text": "Tehching Hsieh's long-term performances rest on recurring actions. Heinz von Foerster describes recursion as the feeding back of a result into the conditions of the next operation. With Hsieh, too, the next repetition is never entirely without preconditions. Fatigue, missed sleep and the time already passed change the body that has to fulfil the rule once more. The rule remains the same, while its own execution continuously generates new conditions.",
  "question": "What does each repetition change about the next?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__karen-barad": {
  "text": "Felix Gonzalez-Torres' works come into being in an arrangement of material, exhibition space, rules and actions of the audience. Karen Barad calls relations in which the sides involved are not already fully fixed beforehand intra-actions. A pile of sweets is therefore not simply a finished work with which visitors subsequently do something. Taking and replenishing belong to the conditions under which its respective state and its identity are brought forth.",
  "question": "Who or what produces the work in each case?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "Rebecca Horn connects bodies with feathers, rods, masks and mechanical extensions. Gilbert Simondon thinks of individuation as a process between an individual and its milieu. Horn's apparatuses therefore leave open where the finished body is supposed to begin. New reaches, resistances and movements arise only in the connection. The machine does not simply supplement an already completed organism. Body and apparatus determine themselves anew in their interplay.",
  "question": "Where does the body end when the apparatus comes into being with it?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__gilles-deleuze": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović has repeated her own performances and re-performed historical performances by other artists. Gilles Deleuze understands repetition not as identical return, but as the production of difference. This becomes especially clear with performance. The same sequence meets a different body, a different audience and a different historical situation. Re-performance preserves a form only by bringing forth a new event.",
  "question": "Can a performance recur without becoming the same?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's instructions seem at first only to lay down what is to be executed. Michel Foucault directs attention to how rules at the same time organise positions, responsibilities and visibility. A Wall Drawing makes certain forms visible, while the work of those executing it easily disappears behind the artist's name and the apparent neutrality of the rule. The instruction therefore orders not only lines, but also roles in the process of production.",
  "question": "Who becomes visible through a rule?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__heinz-von-foerster": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović's performances can create situations in which the observers do not remain outside what is happening. Heinz von Foerster's second-order cybernetics directs attention to the observer as part of what is observed. A glance, an approach or a withdrawal by the audience changes the situation, which is then responded to in turn. Observation thereby acquires consequences and returns to the performance as an altered condition.",
  "question": "What happens when observation acts back?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović ties performance to the presence of a body in an unrepeatable present. Jacques Derrida makes visible at the same time that an event can only be passed on if traces of it become repeatable. Photographs, descriptions, videos and reenactments do not rescue the original presence. They create new situations in which the past event returns differently. Precisely the uniqueness of the performance thus produces the conditions of its later repetition.",
  "question": "What remains of a unique present?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's instruction is repeatable without every execution having to be identical. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun describes digital media in terms of repetition, habit and update: something remains functional by being renewed again and again. LeWitt's rule can be read similarly. It does not preserve a finished state, but enables new actualisations of the same work. Stability arises from the capacity to be executed again.",
  "question": "When does a rule become an update?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__judith-butler": {
  "text": "Tehching Hsieh subjects himself to an explicitly formulated rule whose repetition orders his behaviour over months or years. Judith Butler, by contrast, examines norms that are often effective precisely because they do not lie before us like rules. They become self-evident through repeated actions. Hsieh's performance makes this difference visible: his rule is artificial, dated and terminable, yet its continued execution begins to structure everyday life and body like a norm.",
  "question": "When does a rule begin to act like a norm?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's instructions can give the impression that a rule is independent of who executes it. Donna Haraway contradicts the notion of a knowledge without standpoint. Even the most precise instruction is read, measured out and implemented by particular bodies in particular places. Differences in space, material and experience do not disappear behind the rule. The seemingly general instruction receives a concrete, situated form every time.",
  "question": "From what place is a rule executed?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin's lines divide the picture surface, yet their effect depends heavily on distance, light and attention. George Spencer-Brown begins form with the drawing of a distinction. What is interesting with Martin is how little such a distinction needs. A barely visible line can generate two areas, but only for a gaze that perceives it at all. Boundary and observation arise here almost in the same moment.",
  "question": "When does a line become a boundary?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović often exposes bodies to conditions that are publicly observable. Michel Foucault examines visibility not merely as seeing, but as a component of arrangements in which behaviour is guided and judged. In a performance, the audience therefore does not only change the attention. Its presence belongs to the situation in which endurance, rule-following and transgression acquire meaning. The gaze is no invisible outside, but a condition of the execution.",
  "question": "When does visibility become part of the action?"
 },
 "bruce-nauman__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "Bruce Nauman develops simple tasks, movements and spatial arrangements that quickly become more complicated in execution. Lucy Suchman shows that a plan does not fully contain an action. Bodies have to respond to space, resistance, balance and unexpected situations. Nauman's repetitions make exactly this difference visible. The instruction remains brief, while each execution demands new small decisions.",
  "question": "What remains of a plan in the body?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "Tehching Hsieh's time rules seem at first entirely general: an hour is sixty minutes for everyone. Donna Haraway reminds us that all knowledge and all experience is situated. For Hsieh's body, the same hour means something different depending on sleep, fatigue, weather and what has gone before. The clock establishes equality while the body experiences difference. Precisely this tension makes measurable time visible as a concrete condition of life.",
  "question": "Whose time does the same clock measure?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "Doris Salcedo works with material traces of violence and loss without simply replacing the absent with images. Michel Foucault examines orders of visibility and the conditions under which something can appear. Salcedo's works therefore do not merely exhibit memories. They organise a form of seeing in which gap, empty place and damaged material become legible. Absence becomes visible without thereby being cancelled.",
  "question": "How does absence become visible without being cancelled?"
 },
 "james-turrell__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "James Turrell plans rooms, openings and lighting conditions with great precision. Lucy Suchman shows, however, that planned procedures only become actual actions in concrete situations. Perception, too, cannot be fully pre-programmed. Eyes adapt at different speeds, people move, wait or overlook something. Turrell can design conditions of seeing, but not the individual act of seeing itself. The work therefore arises between spatial planning and situated execution.",
  "question": "Can perception be planned?"
 },
 "christian-marclay__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "Christian Marclay breaks films down into fragments that each show or name a time of day. Claude Shannon separates the signal from its meaning in the transmission of a message. In The Clock, such signals from entirely different stories are referred to a shared temporal order. Only the synchronisation with the actual time of the audience turns heterogeneous scenes into a shared now. The technical legibility of time thus becomes the precondition of a common present.",
  "question": "When does information become shared time?"
 },
 "roman-opalka__gilles-deleuze": {
  "text": "Roman Opalka repeats the same elementary operation over decades: one number follows the next. Gilles Deleuze understands repetition not as the return of the same but as the production of difference. Opalka's series makes this tension extremely visible. The procedure remains almost identical, yet no number and no moment can ever recur. It is precisely repetition that generates a sequence in which each new step widens the distance from the beginning.",
  "question": "Can repetition exist without return?"
 },
 "james-turrell__heinz-von-foerster": {
  "text": "James Turrell directs attention not only to light but to the act of seeing itself. Heinz von Foerster is interested in second-order observation, that is, in observing an observer in the act of observing. With Turrell, this second level can fold back into one's own perception. One sees a field of light and notices at the same time how one's own eye produces depth, edge or colour. Perception becomes its own object.",
  "question": "Can seeing observe itself?"
 },
 "on-kawara__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "On Kawara's date paintings look like pure information: a date, standardised lettering, a plain ground. N. Katherine Hayles, however, contradicts the notion that information could exist independently of its embodiment. Kawara's date is painted, on a particular day, on a particular canvas and under a rule that demands bodily work before midnight. The abstract calendar code remains bound to material and to lifetime.",
  "question": "How much body is there in a date?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "William Kentridge erases his charcoal drawings without letting their earlier states disappear completely. Jacques Derrida's trace designates a presence that remains marked by something absent. With Kentridge, what has been erased can therefore have a stronger effect than an untouched surface. Every remnant points to an image that is no longer there and yet alters the next drawing. Erasing removes a form and at the same time produces its trace.",
  "question": "Can erasure strengthen a trace?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "Tehching Hsieh organises a year through rules that recur exactly. Henri Bergson distinguishes measurable time from duration, which is not composed of identical units. A calendar can break Hsieh's year down into days and hours, but the body lives through no hour like any other. Fatigue, memory and expectation alter duration from within. The rule makes time uniform and precisely thereby allows its unevenness to be experienced.",
  "question": "What remains of duration when every day is counted?"
 },
 "roman-opalka__judith-butler": {
  "text": "Roman Opalka binds his life's work to a simple, self-imposed rule: one number follows the next. Judith Butler examines a different kind of binding. Social forms gain stability because actions are repeated and can thereby come to seem self-evident. With Opalka the rule lies open to view, yet it too becomes a form of life only through countless repetitions. The single number fulfils an instruction, while the continued practice binds the artist and his work ever more closely to one another.",
  "question": "When does repetition become an obligation?"
 },
 "donald-judd__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Donald Judd's works often appear as autonomous, precisely placed bodies. Susan Leigh Star, by contrast, directs attention to the infrastructures that make stability possible in the background. Judd's precision, too, depends on less visible conditions: fabrication, dimensions, wall, floor, fixings and repeatable modes of installation. The gap between two elements is therefore not simply empty space. It has to be produced anew, technically and institutionally, at every presentation.",
  "question": "What keeps a precise gap in place?"
 },
 "dan-graham__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "Dan Graham's glass, mirrors and cameras distribute visibility among people who observe one another and at the same time become visible themselves. Michel Foucault examines arrangements in which seeing is not neutral but organises positions and behaviour. Graham's spaces offer no secure external standpoint. Whoever looks in may become an image for others. Transparency therefore does not simply mean more visibility, but a particular order of mutual observation.",
  "question": "Who sees whom in a transparent space?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "Felix Gonzalez-Torres lets works change their material states continuously. Sweets disappear, stacks of paper shrink and can be replenished. Gilbert Simondon understands individuation not as the production of a definitive individual but as a process that keeps further changes open. The work would then not be a fixed form behind changing states. Its identity consists in the way it can go on individuating itself through these states.",
  "question": "When does change become the form of identity?"
 },
 "bruce-nauman__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "Bruce Nauman repeats words, gestures and movements until deliberate execution can pass over into rhythm or habit. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun is interested in how repetition stabilises behaviour and media by letting the past go on working in present routines. With Nauman, the body carries every previous repetition into the next. What looks like the same act therefore already possesses a memory of practice, fatigue and expectation.",
  "question": "When does repetition acquire a memory?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "William Kentridge leaves earlier states of an image standing as smudges and traces of erasure. Gregory Bateson understands information as a difference that makes a further difference. A trace is therefore not informative simply by being present. It becomes so where the comparison with the present image reveals a change. Kentridge's drawing visibly carries its own history of differences with it.",
  "question": "When does a trace become information?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__heinz-von-foerster": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin's grids at first appear as an order that exists independently of the person looking at them. Heinz von Foerster, however, directs attention to the observer, who is the one to designate differences in the first place. With Martin, the picture changes with distance, light and attention. Lines come forward or vanish, regularity tips into deviation. The grid therefore does not only order the surface. It can make visible how one's own gaze produces order.",
  "question": "Can a grid make the observer visible?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven rewrites time in numbers, dates and serial notations. Henri Bergson warns against equating duration with a spatially ordered sequence of measurable units. Darboven's work sharpens precisely this difference. The calendar allows days to be set side by side and counted, while the writing itself consumes time that is not absorbed into the page. The notation orders duration and shows at the same time that duration is more than its ordering.",
  "question": "What does duration lose when it is notated?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "George Spencer-Brown begins with the drawing of a boundary through which two sides come into being. Mona Hatoum makes such separations bodily uncertain. A grille can mean protection or captivity, a threshold access or exclusion. Which side appears as inside or outside therefore depends not only on the line but also on the position of the body in relation to it. The form of the boundary remains simple; its social and spatial meaning does not.",
  "question": "Who stands on which side?"
 },
 "dan-graham__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "Dan Graham's mirrors and transparent pavilions offer no view from nowhere. Every view depends on where a body stands, who is on the other side and which reflections happen to enter the surface at that moment. Donna Haraway's situated knowledge begins precisely with this boundness. Observation thereby loses not its validity but its claim to placelessness. Graham's architecture makes the standpoint a visible component of the image.",
  "question": "Is there a neutral standpoint in the mirror?"
 },
 "christian-marclay__karen-barad": {
  "text": "Christian Marclay's montages appear to join film or sound fragments that already exist. Karen Barad's concept of intra-action, however, calls into question whether the units involved are already fully determined before their relation. A cut does not only create a connection between two fragments. It also determines where one ends, where the other begins and what meaning both acquire in their new neighbourhood.",
  "question": "Does a fragment only come into being through its cut?"
 },
 "robert-smithson__michel-serres": {
  "text": "Robert Smithson's Non-Sites bring rock, maps and containers from a distant place into the exhibition space. Michel Serres is interested in passages, translations and routes along which something changes its relations. With Smithson, the place is not simply transported. Material, map and naming produce a new connection between here and there. Precisely because the site remains absent, it can travel through various mediations.",
  "question": "How far can a place travel?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__gilles-deleuze": {
  "text": "Doris Salcedo uses recurring things such as furniture, items of clothing or architectural interventions to work through experiences of loss and violence. Gilles Deleuze understands repetition not as the production of the same but as difference. With Salcedo, the recurring form must precisely not make individual losses interchangeable. Her repetition can rather show that similar structures of violence inscribe themselves differently in singular histories.",
  "question": "Can repetition make singularity visible?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "Rebecca Horn joins bodies to mechanical extensions whose movement acts back upon the body. Gregory Bateson thinks of systems in terms of feedback loops in which the consequences of an action become the new conditions of acting. With Horn, a prosthesis therefore does not only extend reach. Weight, resistance and reaction alter the next movement. Body and apparatus form a circuit in which each continuously corrects the other.",
  "question": "When does a prosthesis become feedback?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "Claude Shannon treats noise as a disturbance of the most reliable possible transmission of a signal. Eva Hesse's works confront this logic with a problem. Latex discolours, loses tension and alters a form that is supposed to be preserved. Technically the deviation can be described, but Shannon's theory cannot decide whether it is loss or part of the work. With Hesse, it is precisely the boundary between signal and noise that becomes an aesthetic question.",
  "question": "Who decides whether change is noise?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "Tehching Hsieh formulates rules that are meant to hold for a whole year. Lucy Suchman shows that a plan can never fully anticipate concrete action. The longer Hsieh's rule lasts, the more unplanned situations it must pass through: fatigue, weather, illness, encounters and practical obstacles. The plan remains the same, but its survival depends daily on situated coordination.",
  "question": "How does a plan survive a year?"
 },
 "roman-opalka__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "Roman Opalka writes a number and moves irrevocably on to the next. Jacques Derrida's trace designates something present that remains co-determined by what is absent. With Opalka, every number therefore stands in a field of what has already been written and what has not yet been written. The previous number has vanished and nevertheless remains the condition of the next. The series does not carry its past alongside itself, but in every further step.",
  "question": "Where does the number that has just passed remain?"
 },
 "james-turrell__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "James Turrell works with light that can only become depth, surface or coloured space through a perceiving body. N. Katherine Hayles criticises the notion of an information that would be independent of its embodiment. Light, too, can be described physically, but Turrell's work does not consist in these data alone. Eyes, adaptation, posture and space belong to the conditions of what appears at all.",
  "question": "Can light be information without a body?"
 },
 "bruce-nauman__judith-butler": {
  "text": "Bruce Nauman repeats bodily postures and everyday movements until they appear artificial, strenuous or strange. Judith Butler examines how norms make bodies legible and behaviour self-evident through repeated actions. Nauman can disturb such self-evidence by isolating and exaggerating an ordinary movement. What seems normal suddenly shows the repetitions through which it became normal.",
  "question": "When does a posture become normal?"
 },
 "on-kawara__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "On Kawara's Today Series consists of individual date paintings, each of which may only be made on the day it names. Gilbert Simondon understands individuation as a process out of which a unity first emerges. Kawara's series, too, is not simply the sum of finished paintings. With each new date, what this ongoing work is as a whole changes. The identity of the series grows out of events that remain individually completed and jointly incomplete.",
  "question": "When does a series become a work?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin's grids order a surface with a few recurring lines. Michel Foucault is interested in orders that do not merely sort things but determine which differences become visible and comparable at all. Martin's grid imposes no single reading. Yet against its background, minimal deviations, interruptions and intensities first become noticeable. The order thereby produces a field of what can appear as a difference.",
  "question": "What may appear as deviation within the grid?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__gilles-deleuze": {
  "text": "Louise Bourgeois returns over decades to spiders, houses, body parts and family motifs. Gilles Deleuze understands repetition as the production of difference. Bourgeois' return to the same motifs therefore does not lead to a stable memory. Material, scale and mood shift, and with them the past that is summoned in the form changes. Memory does not merely repeat. It produces new presents of what has passed.",
  "question": "Why does memory return changed?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__heinz-von-foerster": {
  "text": "Mona Hatoum's maps at first seem to order a world in which the viewer would only need to find their own location. Heinz von Foerster, however, directs attention to the observer, who makes distinctions of their own. With Hatoum, a different location does not necessarily change the map, but it does change what appears on it as proximity, border or connection. The order of the world does not simply lie before the gaze. It becomes legible from a position.",
  "question": "Where does the observer stand in their own map?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__karen-barad": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's instructions at first glance look like abstract rules that subsequently merely have to be executed in material. Karen Barad, however, would not treat matter as the passive carrier of an already finished form. Wall, tool, scale and bodily execution all contribute to which concrete form emerges. The rule remains important, but it does not stand wholly opposed to the material.",
  "question": "Where does the rule end and the wall begin?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "Felix Gonzalez-Torres creates works whose state is co-determined by the bodies and decisions of the audience. Donna Haraway insists that knowledge always arises from concrete positions. The work, too, does not appear the same from every standpoint. Whoever takes a sweet, whoever only looks, whoever replenishes or whoever knows the rules of the exhibition encounters a different situation. Commonality here does not arise through identical experience.",
  "question": "Does every audience have the same work?"
 },
 "christian-marclay__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "Christian Marclay's The Clock arranges thousands of film fragments so that the times they depict coincide with the real time in the exhibition space. Henri Bergson distinguishes such measurable, divisible time from lived duration. Before Marclay's work, the two orders meet. The next minute arrives punctually and has its fixed place, while waiting, suspense or boredom alter its duration. Time is at once precisely set and unevenly experienced.",
  "question": "How long does an ordered minute last?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven transforms dates and sequences of time into writing, numbers and serial records. Jacques Derrida's trace makes clear that writing can keep what is absent present without bringing back its original presence. A notated day is therefore not the past day itself. It remains available as a repeatable sign and can be read again in new contexts. Writing preserves time only by transferring it into another form.",
  "question": "Can writing preserve time without holding it fast?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "William Kentridge draws, erases and updates the same sheet again and again. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun describes digital media as systems whose permanence paradoxically depends on continual updating. Kentridge's analogue technique shows a related logic. The image is not preserved by conserving one state. It stays in motion because every state is overwritten and its traces pass into the next version.",
  "question": "Is erasing a form of updating?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "Eva Hesse's materials change their tension, colour and surface. Gregory Bateson understands information as a difference that triggers a further difference within a context. A small material change therefore only becomes significant where it alters form, perception or the handling of the work. Ageing is not merely a state of the substance. It can become feedback about how strongly form and material depend on one another.",
  "question": "When does material change become information?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "Rebecca Horn's apparatuses extend bodies and at the same time determine which movements become possible, difficult or impossible. Michel Foucault examines arrangements in terms of how they guide bodies without directly commanding every action. Horn's prostheses show this guidance immediately. A technical extension opens up new ranges of reach and at the same time inscribes new limits, postures and paths of movement into the body.",
  "question": "Which movement does an apparatus permit, and which does it prevent?"
 },
 "dan-graham__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Dan Graham's pavilions appear transparent, yet they organise very precisely who can see whom, which routes are open and how public or private a position appears. Susan Leigh Star examines infrastructures as relational orders that enable and limit action. Graham's architecture makes such an order visible. Glass and mirror are not merely material but infrastructure for relations between gazes.",
  "question": "When does architecture become an infrastructure of the gaze?"
 },
 "robert-smithson__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "Robert Smithson's Non-Sites transfer a distant place into the exhibition space through material, map, photograph and naming. Claude Shannon asks which information can be reliably transmitted through a channel. With Smithson, it is precisely what does not come along that becomes visible. Rock can be transported, coordinates can be given and pictures can be shown, yet the site itself remains elsewhere. Mediation makes the place addressable without transmitting it completely.",
  "question": "How much of a place can be transmitted?"
 },
 "marina-abramovic__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "Marina Abramović exposes the body to situations whose duration and conditions change it during the performance. Gilbert Simondon understands individuation as a process, not as the unfolding of an already finished individual. The body therefore does not simply enter a performance and leave it again identical. Fatigue, pain, attention and the relation to the audience form new states out of which the further performance emerges.",
  "question": "What does the body become during a performance?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__michel-serres": {
  "text": "Doris Salcedo lets absent people become present through furniture, fabrics, cracks and other material traces, without replacing them. Michel Serres is interested in mediators and passages that change what is transmitted. In this sense Salcedo's material does not function like a neutral messenger. It carries loss onward by giving it a new form, a different weight and a new place.",
  "question": "How does material carry an absence onward?"
 },
 "roman-opalka__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "Roman Opalka orders his work as an unbroken sequence of numbers. Each number has an unambiguous place, and each new number can be determined as the next step. Henri Bergson distinguishes such countable, spatially ordered time from duration, in which the past goes on working in the present. Opalka's series brings both forms together. The number fixes a position, while the lifetime spent making it is not absorbed into identical positions.",
  "question": "Can a number contain duration?"
 },
 "christian-marclay__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "Christian Marclay works with stored sound and image that can be cut, rearranged and played back again. N. Katherine Hayles criticises the notion that information could exist independently of its particular embodiment. Marclay's fragments, too, change their effect with medium, loudspeaker, projection space and the body present. The material does not merely transport information. It is part of how this information becomes perceptible at all.",
  "question": "Where does information acquire a body?"
 },
 "bruce-nauman__heinz-von-foerster": {
  "text": "Bruce Nauman builds situations in which movements, images or observations come back to the person acting. Heinz von Foerster is interested in second-order observation and in recursive processes that alter their own conditions. As soon as Nauman couples a repetition with a mirror, a camera or feedback, the next execution can no longer be quite the same. The repetition sees its own trace and thereby becomes a different one.",
  "question": "What changes when repetition sees itself?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__gilles-deleuze": {
  "text": "William Kentridge draws, erases and draws again. Gilles Deleuze understands repetition not as the return of the identical but as the production of difference. Kentridge's procedure makes this idea material. The same surface, the same hand and the same figure can recur, yet every repetition meets the remains of the previous one. The drawing does not repeat itself over a neutral present, but on a past that has already entered into it.",
  "question": "How much difference does a repetition leave behind?"
 },
 "james-turrell__karen-barad": {
  "text": "James Turrell produces situations in which light cannot simply be regarded as an object that is present. Space, aperture, eye and bodily adaptation jointly determine what appears as surface, depth or colour. Karen Barad's concept of intra-action directs attention precisely to such phenomena, in which the sides involved are not fully fixed in advance. Perceiving body and field of light become determinable in the event of their relation.",
  "question": "Where does light end and perception begin?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "Felix Gonzalez-Torres can describe a work through certificate, dimensions and rules in such a way that it can be installed anew in different places. Claude Shannon shows how information can be reliably transmitted independently of its meaning. Yet a complete transmission of the specifications does not yet produce the same material situation. Weight, removal, audience and renewal have first to take place. The transmissible code stabilises the work without containing its state at any given moment.",
  "question": "How much of a work can be transmitted as information?"
 },
 "roman-opalka__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "Roman Opalka's work persists only by being continued. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun describes digital permanence as paradoxical: something remains available because it is updated, renewed and executed again and again. Opalka's series shows an analogue form of such permanence. It preserves no state. Each new number changes the whole and precisely thereby keeps the project alive.",
  "question": "Is continuing a form of updating?"
 },
 "on-kawara__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "On Kawara's date paintings give a day an unambiguous place in the calendar. Henri Bergson distinguishes such measurable time from duration, which is not composed of identical units. Kawara's rule brings this difference into a single painting. The day is exactly designated and closed, while the work on it is experienced as fatigue, rhythm and attention. The same time appears at once as a position in the calendar and as a lived course.",
  "question": "How much duration fits into a date?"
 },
 "donald-judd__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "Donald Judd organises bodies and intervals in precise relations. Gregory Bateson understands information as a difference that makes a further difference within a context. A gap in Judd's work is therefore not merely an empty, measurable stretch. It lets two bodies appear as separate, as equal in rank or as serially connected. The information lies not in a single element but in the difference between them.",
  "question": "When does a gap become a difference?"
 },
 "christian-marclay__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "Christian Marclay detaches film and sound fragments from their original contexts and sets them in new sequences. Jacques Derrida's iterability designates the possibility of a sign functioning again in other contexts. Yet precisely this repeatability changes its relations. Marclay's fragment remains recognisable and at the same time becomes something else as soon as cut, neighbourhood and a new rhythm shift its legibility.",
  "question": "What changes a fragment when it returns?"
 },
 "mona-hatoum__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "Mona Hatoum works with grilles, maps, thresholds and domestic forms that organise access and exclusion spatially. Michel Foucault examines arrangements in terms of how they distribute people and structure visibility. With Hatoum, a boundary therefore does not only decide where someone can stand. It also changes who becomes visible, protected or exposed from which position. Spatial order and visibility come into being together.",
  "question": "Who is made visible by a boundary?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__judith-butler": {
  "text": "William Kentridge takes up images of political history, erases them, displaces them and reassembles them. Judith Butler examines the frames through which bodies, violence and loss come to appear as visible and grievable at all. With Kentridge, historical memory is therefore not merely repeated. Changing an image can also change what becomes perceptible in it and whose history becomes legible there. Revision thus concerns not only the past but its present conditions of appearance.",
  "question": "Which past does an altered frame make visible?"
 },
 "dan-graham__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "Dan Graham's video installations and mirrors entwine bodies with technical images of themselves. N. Katherine Hayles criticises an understanding of information that treats the body merely as an interchangeable carrier. With Graham, however, the image cannot be detached from its embodiment. Location, movement, delay and direction of gaze decide which information the screen delivers at all. The body is not in front of the media system but inside its loop.",
  "question": "Where is the body in the image?"
 },
 "doris-salcedo__heinz-von-foerster": {
  "text": "Doris Salcedo's works give loss no closed representation. Empty space, damaged material and blocked room set traces without making the absent themselves available. Heinz von Foerster directs attention to the observer as part of every observation. Memory therefore does not lie fully ready in the object. Only in present observation are relations between trace, absence and history established.",
  "question": "What does observation make of a trace?"
 },
 "robert-smithson__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "Robert Smithson's Site and Non-Site make clear that a place never appears completely from a single position. Map, rock, photograph and exhibition form different approaches to the same site. Donna Haraway's situated knowledge contradicts the claim to a view from nowhere. Smithson's work can therefore be read as a spatial test of this: every approach shows something and leaves other things outside its standpoint.",
  "question": "Can a place be described from nowhere?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven's works grow out of continued operations of writing, calculating and dating. Gilbert Simondon understands individuation as a process in which unity first comes into being and is never fully completed. Darboven's series, too, does not possess its identity before its individual entries. Each new section changes the extent and the relations of the whole. Unity here arises through continued becoming.",
  "question": "How does unity arise from continued writing?"
 },
 "agnes-martin__michel-serres": {
  "text": "Agnes Martin's grids divide the surface into lines and intervals without prescribing a fixed route for the gaze. Michel Serres is interested in routes, neighbourhoods and passages that cannot be reduced to metric distance. With Martin, too, the shortest geometric connection is not necessarily the path of perception. The gaze jumps, lingers, loses lines and finds them again. A spatial order thus becomes a field of possible movements.",
  "question": "How does the gaze wander through a grid?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "Eva Hesse's materials change even when the work is to be preserved as far as possible. Jacques Derrida's trace designates something present that remains marked by what is no longer fully present. Ageing latex can make such a temporality materially visible. The earlier state is lost but goes on working in the present work as comparison, documentation and change. Decay does not remove the past; it inscribes it into the surface.",
  "question": "Is decay a trace?"
 },
 "dan-graham__karen-barad": {
  "text": "Dan Graham's mirrors and transparent surfaces make it difficult to separate observer and observed scene cleanly from one another. Karen Barad's intra-action begins precisely where the sides involved only become determinable within a relation. In the mirror the observer appears as part of the image, while the image co-defines their position. The boundary between seeing and being seen is not abolished, but produced anew in every situation.",
  "question": "Does the observer only come into being in the mirror?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Tehching Hsieh's time clock performance takes an inconspicuous instrument of labour organisation and makes it the centre of a whole year. Susan Leigh Star examines infrastructures that usually only attract notice when they break down or step out of their background. With Hsieh, precisely this happens with the time clock. A device for administering working hours becomes visible as an order that structures sleep, movement and everyday life.",
  "question": "When does a time clock become the infrastructure of a life?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__gilles-deleuze": {
  "text": "Rebecca Horn's machines repeat movements, yet the repetition meets body, material and space. Gilles Deleuze distinguishes repetition from the mere occurrence of the same. With Horn, a mechanically identical movement can therefore have a different effect every time. Feathers bend, bodies react and distances change. The machine repeats a sequence and precisely thereby produces difference.",
  "question": "What does a machine really repeat?"
 },
 "robert-smithson__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "Robert Smithson's Site and Non-Site exist through a relation between two different places and forms of representation. Gregory Bateson understands information as a difference within a context. The site therefore cannot be understood through the material in the landscape alone, nor the non-site through its map alone. Only their difference generates information about what is missing in each case and what the other side makes visible.",
  "question": "When does one place become legible through another?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__lucy-suchman": {
  "text": "Felix Gonzalez-Torres specifies dimensions, weights or possibilities of renewal for some works without thereby fixing their actual course. Lucy Suchman shows that plans only become actions in concrete situations. In the museum, staff and audience have to decide how things are taken, replenished, protected or presented. The identity of the work therefore arises not from the rule alone but from its continued situated execution.",
  "question": "Who executes a work that is allowed to change?"
 },
 "louise-bourgeois__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "Louise Bourgeois translates biographical experiences again and again into spaces, bodies and recurring motifs. Claude Shannon can describe how signs are reliably transmitted, but not what personal meaning they carry. It is precisely this limit that is productive in Bourgeois' work. A spider, a house or a body part can be visibly passed on without the memory from which the form emerged being transmitted along with it.",
  "question": "How much memory can be transmitted?"
 },
 "donald-judd__michel-foucault": {
  "text": "Donald Judd's objects order the exhibition space through dimensions, intervals and recurring placements. Michel Foucault examines spatial orders in terms of how they structure positions and possibilities of seeing. Judd's work prescribes no single direction of gaze, but it changes where a body can stand and which relations become visible at the same time. Geometric order thereby also becomes an order of possible observations.",
  "question": "Do intervals become rules of seeing?"
 },
 "christian-marclay__gilbert-simondon": {
  "text": "Christian Marclay breaks down existing material and joins fragments into new temporal units. Gilbert Simondon understands individuation as a process in which a unity emerges from relations and tensions. A montage would then not merely be the sum of already finished parts. Rhythm, cut and neighbourhood change what the fragments are and at the same time let a new form emerge that was previously contained in no single fragment.",
  "question": "When do fragments become a new unity?"
 },
 "on-kawara__judith-butler": {
  "text": "On Kawara repeats the making of date paintings according to a strict rule over years. Judith Butler shows how repetition can stabilise forms and make them socially legible. Kawara's date is itself a culturally shared convention whose legibility rests on continued repetition. At the same time, each painting designates an unrepeatable day. The work joins a repeatable social form to an event that takes place only once.",
  "question": "How does repetition make a unique day legible?"
 },
 "james-turrell__michel-serres": {
  "text": "James Turrell lets light enter spaces of perception through apertures, thresholds and architectural transitions. Michel Serres is interested in passages in which what is mediated is changed by its route. Light, too, does not simply reach the gaze unmediated. Aperture, atmosphere, wall and eye jointly determine how it appears. The path of the light thus belongs to its perceived form.",
  "question": "When does light become space along its way?"
 },
 "tehching-hsieh__n-katherine-hayles": {
  "text": "Tehching Hsieh's rules can be stated briefly, yet their meaning only arises through a body carrying them out over months or years. N. Katherine Hayles criticises the notion that information could be separated from its embodiment. With Hsieh, the rule is transmissible, but the performance is not. Lack of sleep, weight, weather and exhaustion inscribe themselves into an enactment that no description can fully replace.",
  "question": "Can a body store a rule?"
 },
 "bruce-nauman__gregory-bateson": {
  "text": "Bruce Nauman works with repetitions, feedback and situations in which an action acts back upon the person performing it. Gregory Bateson describes systems in terms of differences and feedback loops. A loop is therefore more than the return of the same signal as soon as its result changes further behaviour. With Nauman, precisely this transition can turn a simple repetition into a system.",
  "question": "When does a loop become feedback?"
 },
 "sol-lewitt__susan-leigh-star": {
  "text": "Sol LeWitt's instructions allow a work to be executed in different places independently of his own hand. Susan Leigh Star directs attention to the infrastructures that make such apparent self-evidence possible. Those who execute it, wall preparation, documentation, material, rights and institutional knowledge form a network without which the rule does not become a work. The idea appears autonomous because its infrastructure works reliably.",
  "question": "Which infrastructure executes an idea?"
 },
 "william-kentridge__henri-bergson": {
  "text": "William Kentridge lets earlier states of a drawing live on as traces in the present image. Henri Bergson understands memory not as an archive filed away beside the present but as something that acts into present perception and action. Kentridge's sheet gives this thought a material form. The past is not merely documented. It changes what can be drawn now.",
  "question": "How much past does an image carry?"
 },
 "roman-opalka__claude-shannon": {
  "text": "Roman Opalka's sequence of numbers can be read as extremely precise information: each number unambiguously designates its place in the series. Claude Shannon can describe how such distinguishable signs are transmitted. Yet the number does not transmit the lifetime, bodily work and attention that its making cost. Opalka's work thus lays bare a difference between an exactly codable order and irreversibly spent time.",
  "question": "What does a number say about the time it cost?"
 },
 "rebecca-horn__jacques-derrida": {
  "text": "Rebecca Horn supplements bodies with feathers, rods, masks and mechanical extensions. Jacques Derrida's concept of the supplement designates an addition that at the same time makes visible that the supposedly complete whole was never entirely closed in itself. Horn's prostheses add capacities to the body and thereby shift its boundary. The extension does not only show what more a body can do; it calls into question where this body actually ended before.",
  "question": "Where does a body end that can be supplemented?"
 },
 "eva-hesse__george-spencer-brown": {
  "text": "George Spencer-Brown begins form with a boundary that distinguishes two sides. Eva Hesse's soft, hanging and ageing materials call the stability of such a boundary into question. A contour can sag, lose tension or appear different through material change. The form does not necessarily vanish as a result. Rather, it shows that a boundary can persist without being unchangeable.",
  "question": "How stable must a boundary be to form a form?"
 },
 "donald-judd__donna-haraway": {
  "text": "Donald Judd's spatial orders appear precise and independent of the individual gaze. Donna Haraway, however, contradicts the idea of a seeing without standpoint. With Judd, too, the position of the body changes which intervals overlap, which sides vanish and which relations can be grasped at the same time. The order persists, but it is never completely given from a single place.",
  "question": "Where does the body stand in relation to a precise order?"
 },
 "on-kawara__karen-barad": {
  "text": "On Kawara paints a date only on the day it designates. Karen Barad understands apparatuses not as neutral instruments but as material-discursive arrangements through which particular differences are brought forth in the first place. With Kawara, calendar, clock, painting materials and working rule act together. They do not depict the day as such, but they cut a particular artistic event out of continuous time: this painting as this day.",
  "question": "What does a procedure make of a day?"
 },
 "felix-gonzalez-torres__wendy-hui-kyong-chun": {
  "text": "Felix Gonzalez-Torres preserves some works precisely by having their material stock renewed. Wendy Hui Kyong Chun describes permanence in digital media in terms of repetition and update. A similar tension is found here in analogue form. The work does not persist because its state is frozen, but because removal and replenishment can be continued. Renewal becomes the condition of its continuity.",
  "question": "Is renewing a form of remembering?"
 },
 "hanne-darboven__heinz-von-foerster": {
  "text": "Hanne Darboven produces serial orders from dates, numbers and recurring operations. Heinz von Foerster distinguishes observation from the observation of observing and thereby directs attention to the distinctions themselves that are being used. Darboven's series do not only make results visible. Whoever follows them can at the same time observe by which rule time is ordered and written onward. The order shows something and exhibits its own procedure along with it.",
  "question": "When does the rule itself become observable?"
 },
 "james-turrell__gilles-deleuze": {
  "text": "James Turrell can shape conditions of light so that an apparently identical surface appears different in the course of perception. Gilles Deleuze understands repetition as the production of difference. In seeing, this difference becomes bodily: adaptation, attention and duration change what emerges as colour, depth or edge. The same light therefore cannot simply return unchanged for the same gaze.",
  "question": "Can the same light appear twice?"
 }
};
